LitSearch
A Living Methods Paper for Reproducible Literature Retrieval

Document type: Living Methods Paper
Author / Organization: BibQPrompt
Website: https://bibqprompt.com
Version: 1.3
Date: October 2026
Status: Public methodological documentation (living document)
Scope: PubMed • Europe PMC • OpenAlex • Semantic Scholar • Crossref • DOAJ • All Sources aggregation
Core Architecture: FastAPI • Redis • ARQ • Docker
Methodological Focus: Reproducible literature retrieval, multi-source aggregation, canonical normalization, deterministic deduplication, metadata enrichment, and transparent export workflows

________________________________________
Abstract (stable methodological core)
Reproducibility and transparency remain persistent challenges in literature searching, as many searches are still conducted manually through graphical database interfaces. This limits the ability to fully document, reconstruct, validate, and independently evaluate search procedures.
To address these challenges, LitSearch was developed as a web-based platform for reproducible and transparent literature retrieval. LitSearch formalizes literature searching as an explicit computational workflow in which query construction, filtering, pagination, sorting, aggregation, deduplication, and export generation are represented as explicit and auditable processes.
The current implementation integrates PubMed, Europe PMC, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Crossref, and DOAJ as primary literature retrieval sources. Searches are constructed using combinations of free-text terms and explicit filtering parameters according to the capabilities of each source and retrieval mode. These capabilities include publication-year filtering, abstract-availability filtering, sorting, pagination, and controlled-vocabulary refinement where supported. PubMed and Europe PMC support Medical Subject Headings (MeSH) refinement with explicit AND and OR combination semantics.
In addition to source-specific retrieval, LitSearch supports a multi-source aggregation workflow ("All Sources") that retrieves bounded candidate sets from the six integrated retrieval sources. Retrieved records are normalized into a canonical internal data model and undergo deterministic cross-source deduplication. Persistent identifiers are prioritized where available, including DOI, PMID, and PMCID, while normalized bibliographic metadata provide additional matching mechanisms subject to identifier-conflict safeguards. When duplicate records are matched, available metadata can be merged while preserving source provenance.
Final result ordering is applied centrally within the All Sources workflow. Chronological modes use publication-year ordering, whereas relevance-oriented presentation uses deterministic source-balanced interleaving rather than a common cross-source relevance score. Temporary Redis-backed snapshots preserve the processed and ordered federated result state for subsequent pagination within a configurable retention period, reducing short-term pagination drift without constituting permanent archival preservation of search results.
The platform is implemented as a containerized application using Docker. A FastAPI backend orchestrates query construction, API interaction, pagination, normalization, aggregation, deduplication, and export generation. Redis supports pagination-related operational state, temporary All Sources snapshots, and task coordination, while ARQ workers support asynchronous export workflows.
The canonical Paper model additionally supports metadata enrichment without introducing new search results. In the current implementation, enrichment is applied to individual paper-detail workflows after source-specific retrieval. PubMed-derived MeSH metadata and OpenCitations citation and reference counts can augment existing normalized records where applicable, with field-level enrichment provenance retained in the internal data model. Enrichment remains methodologically distinct from primary literature retrieval.
Validation combines automated regression testing, connector-level verification, integration testing, export validation, and system-level sanity checks. These procedures evaluate source-specific retrieval, pagination, normalization, filtering, aggregation, deduplication, enrichment, export behavior, and user-interface integration. Because LitSearch depends on external scholarly APIs whose content and operational behavior may change over time, reproducibility refers primarily to explicit and reconstructable retrieval procedures rather than a guarantee that external databases will return permanently identical records. This living methods paper documents the stable methodological foundations of LitSearch while permitting controlled extension to additional literature sources, enrichment providers, retrieval capabilities, and export workflows without compromising transparency, provenance, or methodological consistency.
Keywords: Reproducible research; Literature search methodology; Multi-source retrieval; Cross-source deduplication; Metadata enrichment; PubMed; Europe PMC; OpenAlex; Semantic Scholar; Crossref; DOAJ; OpenCitations; Medical Subject Headings (MeSH); Evidence synthesis support

________________________________________
1. Introduction (Stable Core)
Transparent and reproducible literature retrieval is a fundamental requirement for reliable scientific research. Literature searches form the basis of systematic reviews, scoping reviews, evidence syntheses, guideline development, and many other forms of scholarly inquiry. Despite extensive methodological guidance, literature retrieval is frequently conducted through graphical database interfaces, which can make precise documentation, reconstruction, and evaluation of search procedures difficult.
LitSearch was developed to address these limitations by treating literature retrieval as a formal, parameterized computational process. Rather than relying on implicit search interactions, the system represents search strategies through explicit query parameters, filtering rules, sorting options, pagination states, and controlled-vocabulary refinements where supported. This approach preserves interactive exploration while ensuring that retrieval workflows remain transparent, auditable, and reproducible at the procedural level.
A central methodological principle of LitSearch is the separation of retrieval methodology from source-specific implementation details. Literature databases differ in indexing practices, metadata structures, query syntax, pagination mechanisms, sorting behavior, filtering capabilities, and API constraints, yet the underlying retrieval workflow remains consistent. Retrieved records are normalized into a canonical representation, allowing heterogeneous sources to be combined, inspected, deduplicated, exported, and validated through a unified framework.
The current implementation supports six primary literature retrieval sources: PubMed, Europe PMC, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Crossref, and DOAJ. LitSearch also provides a multi-source aggregation workflow ("All Sources") that retrieves candidate records from these sources and combines them into a unified result set. Cross-source deduplication, metadata merging, deterministic result ordering, and standardized export generation are applied through centralized procedures designed to preserve methodological transparency and consistency across heterogeneous sources.
LitSearch additionally distinguishes primary literature retrieval from metadata enrichment. Retrieval sources determine which records enter a result set, whereas enrichment providers may augment an already retrieved and normalized record with additional metadata without introducing new search results. This separation allows enrichment functionality to evolve independently from the primary retrieval methodology while preserving the provenance of added metadata.
This document is intended as a living methods paper. The methodological principles described herein are designed to remain stable even as additional literature sources, enrichment providers, retrieval capabilities, and export workflows are introduced. Future implementation changes may expand functionality, but they should not alter the core principles of explicit parameterization, deterministic processing, transparent provenance, controlled extension, and reproducible literature retrieval.
LitSearch is intended to support literature identification and retrieval as part of broader evidence-synthesis methodologies. The platform does not replace expert screening, critical appraisal, study selection, risk-of-bias assessment, or evidence synthesis. It does not interpret the scientific findings of retrieved papers. Rather, LitSearch provides a reproducible and transparent methodological foundation for the literature retrieval phase of scientific research.

________________________________________
2. System Architecture (Stable Core)
LitSearch follows a modular architecture designed to separate methodological responsibilities while preserving reproducibility, extensibility, and source-specific behavior.
At a conceptual level, the system consists of the following functional layers:
1. Retrieval layer — communicates with external scholarly literature APIs and retrieves source-specific records.
2. Query orchestration layer — translates user-selected search parameters into source-specific retrieval requests while respecting the capabilities and constraints of each source and retrieval mode.
3. Normalization layer — converts heterogeneous source records into a shared canonical Paper representation.
4. Aggregation layer — combines records retrieved from multiple sources, performs deterministic cross-source deduplication and metadata merging, and applies centralized result ordering for All Sources searches.
5. Metadata enrichment layer — augments already retrieved and normalized records with additional metadata without introducing new search results.
6. Export layer — transforms retrieved records into standardized CSV, RIS, and XLSX outputs while preserving the search context required for consistent export generation.
A presentation layer exposes these capabilities through a web interface while preserving explicit search parameters and source-specific navigation behavior.

2.1 Application Components
The current implementation uses:
• FastAPI for HTTP request handling and application routing
• Jinja2 for server-rendered HTML templates
• Redis for pagination-related operational state, temporary All Sources snapshots, and asynchronous task coordination
• ARQ workers for asynchronous export workflows
• source-specific connectors for communication with external scholarly APIs
• a centralized connector-capability registry for representing supported sorting, pagination, navigation, and filtering behavior
• a canonical Paper model for normalized bibliographic records
• a generic enrichment pipeline for controlled augmentation of normalized records

The primary retrieval sources are:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC
• OpenAlex
• Semantic Scholar
• Crossref
• DOAJ

Metadata enrichment is methodologically separate from primary retrieval. In the current implementation, enrichment providers include PubMed-derived MeSH enrichment and OpenCitations citation and reference metadata for applicable paper-detail workflows.

2.2 Canonical Record Model
Retrieved records are transformed into a canonical Paper representation before downstream processing.
The canonical model provides a stable internal interface across heterogeneous literature sources and supports:
• consistent rendering
• cross-source deduplication
• metadata merging
• sorting and filtering workflows
• export generation
• detail-page presentation
• metadata enrichment
The canonical model is therefore independent of the original response structure of any individual upstream API.

2.3 Source Capabilities
External scholarly APIs do not expose identical retrieval functionality. LitSearch therefore models search capabilities explicitly at source level and, where necessary, at retrieval-mode level.
These capabilities include:
• pagination strategy
• supported sort modes
• previous-page navigation
• last-page navigation
• direct page jumping
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• MeSH filtering where supported

The capability model prevents the user interface and routing logic from assuming that all retrieval sources support equivalent operations.

LitSearch currently distinguishes four pagination types:
• page-based pagination
• cursor-based pagination
• token-based pagination
• snapshot-based pagination

The exact strategy depends on the selected source and, for Semantic Scholar, the active retrieval mode.

2.4 Aggregation, Deduplication, and Ordering
For All Sources searches, LitSearch retrieves bounded candidate sets from the integrated primary retrieval sources and combines them into a common processing pipeline.
The aggregation workflow consists conceptually of:
1. source-specific candidate retrieval
2. canonical normalization
3. cross-source duplicate detection
4. metadata merging for matched records
5. centralized result ordering
6. temporary snapshot storage
7. stable page slicing from the processed result state

Cross-source deduplication prioritizes normalized persistent identifiers, including DOI, PMID, and PMCID. Where direct identifier matching is unavailable, normalized bibliographic metadata may provide additional matching mechanisms subject to identifier-conflict safeguards.
When records are identified as duplicates, LitSearch preserves existing metadata where available, fills missing values from matched records, and combines source provenance.
For chronological All Sources modes, result ordering is applied centrally using normalized publication-year metadata.
For relevance-oriented All Sources results, LitSearch does not compute a universal relevance score across databases. Instead, source-specific candidate ordering is retained and deduplicated records are interleaved through a deterministic round-robin procedure using a fixed source order.

2.5 Temporary All Sources Snapshots
Federated pagination introduces a reproducibility challenge because repeated retrieval from multiple external APIs can produce changing candidate sets between page requests.
To reduce this short-term pagination drift, LitSearch can store the processed All Sources result set as a temporary Redis-backed snapshot.
A snapshot contains:
• the processed and ordered Paper records
• the number of duplicates removed
• per-source candidate counts
• information about sources that failed during retrieval
• a signature representing the associated search state

The snapshot signature incorporates the query, normalized sort mode, candidate-set size, publication-year filters, abstract filter, and MeSH parameters.
While a compatible snapshot remains available, subsequent pages are derived from the same processed result state rather than requiring a complete federated retrieval for every page request.
The default snapshot retention period is 900 seconds (15 minutes) and can be configured through the application environment.
Snapshots improve short-term pagination consistency but are not permanent archives of search results. After expiration, a repeated search may produce different records because external literature databases and APIs can change over time.

2.6 Metadata Enrichment
Metadata enrichment is separated from primary literature retrieval.
Retrieval connectors determine which records enter a result set. Enrichment providers operate on records that have already been retrieved and normalized and may add metadata without adding new literature records.
The enrichment pipeline is designed so that provider-specific failures do not invalidate the underlying retrieved record.
In the current paper-detail workflow, source-specific detail retrieval first produces a canonical Paper record. The record may then be processed by configured enrichment providers before presentation.
Current enrichment functionality includes:
• PubMed-derived MeSH metadata enrichment
• OpenCitations citation counts
• OpenCitations reference counts

OpenCitations enrichment requires an identifiable DOI. Citation and reference counts are added independently when available.
Enrichment provenance is retained through the enrichment_sources mapping for fields that are modified by an enrichment provider.
Enrichment is currently associated with individual paper-detail processing and should not be interpreted as a mandatory processing stage for every search-result list or export.

2.7 Operational State
LitSearch uses Redis-backed operational state to support pagination continuity, temporary federated result state, and asynchronous task coordination.
Stateful information can include:
• pagination-related state
• cursor- or token-related pagination state where required
• temporary All Sources snapshots
• export workflow state
• asynchronous job metadata

State identifiers are derived from normalized retrieval parameters where applicable.
Temporary operational state and snapshots support consistent application behavior, but they do not replace permanent archival storage of search strategies or external source content.

2.8 Configuration
Source credentials, API configuration, rate-limit-related settings, cache behavior, snapshot retention, and other operational parameters are provided through environment variables.
This separates deployment-specific configuration from retrieval methodology and application code.
The same application architecture can therefore be deployed across environments while preserving the methodological structure of LitSearch.

________________________________________
3. Data Sources

LitSearch integrates multiple scholarly literature sources through source-specific connectors. Each connector translates the shared LitSearch retrieval model into the query syntax, filtering mechanisms, pagination strategy, sorting behavior, and metadata structure supported by the corresponding upstream API.

The current implementation contains six primary retrieval sources:

• PubMed
• Europe PMC
• OpenAlex
• Semantic Scholar
• Crossref
• DOAJ

These sources remain methodologically distinct. LitSearch does not assume that equivalent filters, sort modes, pagination operations, or metadata fields are available from every source.

3.1 PubMed

PubMed provides biomedical literature retrieval through the NCBI E-utilities APIs.
LitSearch uses:
• ESearch for identifier retrieval
• EFetch for detailed metadata retrieval

The PubMed connector supports:
• free-text search
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• MeSH-based refinement
• explicit AND/OR combination of multiple MeSH terms
• relevance ordering
• newest-first publication-date ordering
• page-oriented navigation within the LitSearch interface

For query construction, LitSearch combines the free-text query with applicable filters using explicit Boolean expressions.
Publication-year restrictions are represented using the PubMed Date - Publication field. Abstract filtering uses the PubMed hasabstract text filter. MeSH descriptors are represented using the MeSH Terms field.
When multiple MeSH terms are supplied, the selected MeSH mode determines whether those terms are combined using AND or OR. The resulting MeSH expression is then combined with the base query and other active filters using AND.
PubMed does not provide oldest-first publication-date sorting through the current LitSearch integration. Unsupported sort requests are therefore normalized to a supported retrieval mode rather than being presented as equivalent behavior.
PubMed records are normalized into the canonical Paper representation before downstream processing.

3.2 Europe PMC
Europe PMC provides biomedical literature metadata through its REST API.
The Europe PMC connector supports:
• free-text search
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• MeSH-based refinement
• explicit AND/OR combination of multiple MeSH terms
• cursor-based retrieval
• relevance-oriented ordering in the current LitSearch integration

LitSearch constructs Europe PMC queries by combining the free-text expression with source-specific filters.

Publication-year filtering is represented using:
PUB_YEAR:[minimum TO maximum]
abstract availability is represented using:
HAS_ABSTRACT:Y
MeSH terms are represented using:
MESH:"term"

When multiple MeSH terms are supplied, LitSearch combines them using the selected AND or OR mode. The resulting MeSH expression is combined with the free-text query and other active filters using AND.
Europe PMC pagination uses cursor-based retrieval. LitSearch maintains the cursor-related state required for navigation while presenting the results through the common web interface.
The current LitSearch integration exposes relevance sorting for Europe PMC. Other user-facing sort requests are not treated as equivalent Europe PMC operations.
Europe PMC records are normalized into the canonical Paper representation before aggregation, export, or detail processing.

3.3 OpenAlex
OpenAlex provides multidisciplinary scholarly metadata through its public API.
The OpenAlex connector supports:
• free-text search
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• relevance ordering
• newest-first chronological ordering
• oldest-first chronological ordering
• page-oriented navigation in the LitSearch interface

Abstract-availability filtering is applied through the native OpenAlex has_abstract filter.
OpenAlex does not support LitSearch MeSH refinement. Source-specific concepts or subject metadata may nevertheless be normalized into the canonical record where available.
OpenAlex records participate in All Sources aggregation and centralized cross-source deduplication.

3.4 Semantic Scholar
Semantic Scholar provides multidisciplinary scholarly metadata through the Semantic Scholar Graph API.
LitSearch distinguishes two Semantic Scholar retrieval modes because relevance-oriented retrieval and chronological bulk retrieval expose different API behavior.
Relevance mode supports:
• relevance-oriented search
• offset/page-based retrieval
• abstract-availability filtering within the LitSearch retrieval workflow
• previous-page navigation

In the current implementation, abstract-availability filtering in relevance mode is applied locally to the records returned by the upstream request.
Publication-year filtering is not exposed as a full-result-set capability for Semantic Scholar relevance mode because the current connector applies year restrictions locally after retrieval rather than as an upstream search constraint.
Relevance mode does not expose direct last-page navigation or arbitrary page jumping in the current LitSearch interface.

Bulk mode supports:
• chronological newest-first ordering
• chronological oldest-first ordering
• token-based pagination
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering within the LitSearch retrieval workflow

For bulk retrieval, publication-year restrictions are transmitted to the upstream API, whereas abstract-availability filtering is applied locally to returned records.
Bulk pagination uses continuation tokens rather than numeric offsets. Direct last-page navigation and arbitrary page jumping are therefore not exposed.
Semantic Scholar requests are additionally subject to connector-level rate-limit handling and retry behavior to accommodate upstream operational constraints.
Semantic Scholar records are normalized into the canonical Paper representation and can participate in All Sources aggregation and cross-source deduplication.

3.5 Crossref
Crossref provides multidisciplinary bibliographic and DOI-oriented metadata through its REST API.
The Crossref connector supports:
• free-text search
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• relevance ordering
• newest-first chronological ordering
• oldest-first chronological ordering
• page-oriented navigation in the LitSearch interface
• DOI-based detail retrieval

Crossref queries use offset-based API retrieval internally. LitSearch translates the requested page and page size into the corresponding Crossref offset.
Publication-year filters are transmitted using Crossref publication-date filters.
Abstract availability is filtered using the native Crossref has-abstract filter.
Chronological sorting uses Crossref publication-date sorting in ascending or descending order as appropriate.
Crossref does not provide LitSearch MeSH refinement.
Returned metadata are normalized into the canonical Paper representation. Crossref records participate in All Sources aggregation, metadata merging, and centralized cross-source deduplication.

3.6 DOAJ
The Directory of Open Access Journals (DOAJ) is integrated as an additional scholarly retrieval source through the DOAJ article API.
The DOAJ connector supports:
• free-text article search
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• page-based pagination
• relevance/default result ordering
• article-detail retrieval

Publication-year restrictions are incorporated into the DOAJ search expression.
Abstract availability is filtered through an existence constraint on the DOAJ abstract field.
The current LitSearch integration does not expose chronological sort modes for DOAJ. Unsupported sort requests are therefore not treated as equivalent DOAJ operations.
DOAJ does not provide LitSearch MeSH refinement.
Where available, DOAJ subject terms and keywords may be normalized as concepts in the canonical Paper model. Full-text availability is derived from suitable links returned in DOAJ metadata.
DOAJ records participate in All Sources aggregation, metadata merging, and centralized cross-source deduplication.

3.7 All Sources Aggregation
The All Sources workflow combines candidate records from all six primary retrieval sources:
• PubMed
• OpenAlex
• Crossref
• DOAJ
• Europe PMC
• Semantic Scholar

All Sources is a federated retrieval workflow rather than a seventh literature database.
For each federated search, LitSearch requests a bounded candidate set from each participating source. Source retrieval is performed concurrently where possible. A failure in one source does not necessarily invalidate the complete federated operation; LitSearch can retain records returned by the remaining sources and track sources that failed during retrieval.
The combined candidate records are processed through a centralized pipeline:
1. retrieve source-specific candidate sets
2. normalize records into canonical Paper objects
3. combine records into a common candidate collection
4. detect cross-source duplicates
5. merge complementary metadata from matched records
6. apply deterministic result ordering
7. store the processed result state as a temporary snapshot where available
8. derive individual result pages from the processed state

Cross-source duplicate detection prioritizes normalized persistent identifiers:
• DOI
• PMID
• PMCID

When direct identifier matching does not establish a duplicate, LitSearch may additionally compare normalized bibliographic metadata, including title, first author, and publication year.
Metadata-based matching is constrained by identifier-conflict safeguards. Records carrying conflicting persistent identifiers are not merged solely because their descriptive metadata are similar.
A constrained tolerant metadata comparison may accommodate a one-year publication-year discrepancy under additional identifier conditions.
When records are matched, LitSearch preserves existing metadata where available, fills missing values from the matched record, and combines source provenance.

3.8 All Sources Filtering Semantics
Not every source supports every LitSearch filter. Federated filtering must therefore be interpreted according to the capabilities of the participating connectors.
Publication-year and abstract-availability filters are supported across much of the retrieval layer, but implementation strategies may differ between sources.
MeSH refinement is source-specific. In the current implementation, MeSH query parameters can be propagated to PubMed and Europe PMC, which support MeSH-aware retrieval. OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Crossref, and DOAJ do not provide equivalent LitSearch MeSH filtering.
For this reason, MeSH is not presented as a full All Sources result-set filtering capability. Applying a MeSH expression to only a subset of participating sources would not be methodologically equivalent to filtering the complete federated result set.
Similarly, a filter should not be assumed to have identical upstream semantics merely because it is represented by a common LitSearch user-interface control.

3.9 All Sources Ordering
All Sources supports centralized chronological ordering and deterministic relevance-oriented presentation.
For chronological modes, LitSearch sorts the deduplicated records centrally using normalized publication-year metadata, with deterministic secondary ordering where required.
For relevance-oriented All Sources results, LitSearch does not attempt to normalize or compare heterogeneous relevance scores from different literature APIs.
Instead, source-specific ordering is retained within each source bucket and the resulting records are interleaved in deterministic round-robin order.
The current source-interleaving order is:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

This procedure provides deterministic source-balanced presentation without implying that relevance scores from heterogeneous databases are directly comparable.

3.10 Extensible Source Integration Model
The retrieval architecture is designed to support additional scholarly sources without changing the stable methodological core.
A new primary retrieval source should provide, where applicable:
• source-specific query construction
• API communication
• pagination handling
• sort translation
• filter translation
• metadata normalization into Paper objects
• detail retrieval
• error and rate-limit handling
• explicit capability definitions
• validation tests

If a source participates in All Sources, its records should additionally enter the centralized aggregation, deduplication, metadata-merging, ordering, snapshot, and export workflows as appropriate.
Source-specific behavior should remain isolated within connector or capability logic wherever possible.
The shared methodological pipeline should remain:
Query
→ Source-specific retrieval
→ Canonical normalization
→ Optional multi-source aggregation
→ Deduplication and metadata merging where applicable
→ Deterministic ordering
→ Presentation or export

Metadata enrichment remains a separate operation on already retrieved canonical records and should not be treated as a primary retrieval source.________________________________________

________________________________________
4. Query Construction and Refinement (Stable Core)
A central methodological principle of LitSearch is that literature retrieval should be represented as an explicit and reproducible computational process. Search strategies are therefore constructed from clearly defined query parameters rather than implicit interactions within graphical user interfaces.
All retrieval workflows begin with a free-text query. Depending on the capabilities of the selected source and retrieval mode, this query may be combined with additional structured parameters.
Supported parameter categories may include:
• free-text search terms
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• source selection
• sorting mode selection
• pagination controls
• controlled-vocabulary refinement where supported

Not every parameter is available for every source or retrieval mode. LitSearch explicitly models these differences rather than assuming uniform capabilities across heterogeneous literature APIs.

4.1 Source-Specific Query Translation
LitSearch maintains a common user-facing retrieval model while translating supported parameters into source-specific API requests.
The same conceptual filter may therefore be implemented differently by different sources. For example, publication-year restrictions, abstract-availability requirements, sorting instructions, and pagination state may be represented through different query expressions or API parameters.
This source-specific translation is intentional. LitSearch seeks to preserve the semantics supported by each upstream source rather than forcing superficially uniform behavior onto APIs with different retrieval models.
A user-facing capability should therefore be interpreted as a LitSearch retrieval capability for the active source and mode, not as evidence that every upstream API implements that capability using the same mechanism.

4.2 Capability-Aware Query Construction
Before exposing or applying retrieval options, LitSearch can use source- and mode-specific capability definitions describing supported behavior.
These definitions cover:
• supported sort modes
• pagination type
• previous-page navigation
• last-page navigation
• direct page jumping
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• MeSH filtering

This capability-aware design reduces the risk that unsupported parameters are silently presented as valid retrieval operations.
Where a requested sort mode is unsupported by the active source or retrieval mode, LitSearch may normalize the request to a supported mode rather than translating it into a semantically different operation.

4.3 Publication-Year Filtering
Publication-year filtering is supported by multiple LitSearch retrieval sources, although its implementation differs between connectors.
Depending on the source, year restrictions may be:
• incorporated into the source query expression
• transmitted as dedicated API filter parameters
• represented as publication-date boundaries
• applied according to retrieval-mode-specific behavior

Because these mechanisms are not methodologically identical, LitSearch preserves source-specific implementation semantics while representing the user's requested year range explicitly.
A year filter should therefore not be interpreted as evidence that all participating literature databases use identical date fields, indexing rules, or publication-date definitions.

4.4 Abstract-Availability Filtering
LitSearch supports abstract-availability filtering for retrieval modes where this capability is exposed.
Depending on the source, abstract filtering may be performed:
• natively by the upstream API
• through a source-specific query expression
• locally on records returned by the upstream API

These approaches can differ in their effect on the complete result set.
In particular, local post-retrieval filtering applies only to records retrieved from the upstream request and is therefore not methodologically equivalent to a server-side constraint applied across the complete upstream result set.
LitSearch documents and models such source-specific differences rather than treating all abstract filters as operationally identical.

4.5 MeSH-Based Query Refinement
Controlled-vocabulary refinement is supported where the retrieval source provides compatible Medical Subject Headings (MeSH) search functionality.
In the current implementation, MeSH-based query refinement is supported for:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC

Slected MeSH terms can be combined using explicit logical semantics:
• AND for conjunctive narrowing
• OR for disjunctive expansion

The selected logical mode determines the relationship among multiple MeSH terms. The resulting MeSH expression is subsequently combined with the free-text query and other applicable filters.
Sources without compatible MeSH retrieval functionality do not receive an equivalent synthetic controlled-vocabulary filter.
This distinction preserves transparent source semantics and avoids presenting source-specific subject concepts or keywords as if they were equivalent to MeSH.

4.6 Multi-Source Query Semantics
For All Sources retrieval, LitSearch distributes the free-text query across participating retrieval sources and applies supported parameters according to the corresponding connector behavior.
The federated workflow should not be interpreted as a single query executed against a homogeneous database. It consists of multiple source-specific retrieval operations followed by centralized processing.
Consequently, a parameter can have different upstream implementations or may be unavailable for some participating sources.
This distinction is particularly important for controlled-vocabulary refinement. MeSH parameters can be propagated to PubMed and Europe PMC, but the remaining primary retrieval sources do not provide equivalent MeSH filtering.
For this reason, LitSearch does not represent MeSH as a full-result-set All Sources filtering capability.

4.7 Explicit Search State
A key reproducibility principle of LitSearch is that retrieval state should remain explicit and reconstructable.
Search state may include:
• the free-text query
• selected source
• active filters
• sort mode
• pagination state
• controlled-vocabulary selections and logical mode
• retrieval-mode-specific state where applicable

Where represented through application URLs and associated retrieval state, these parameters allow search workflows to be documented, shared, inspected, and reconstructed without relying exclusively on screenshots or undocumented user interactions.
For stateful retrieval mechanisms such as cursor-, token-, or snapshot-based pagination, reconstruction may additionally depend on temporary application state or upstream continuation mechanisms. URL parameterization should therefore not be interpreted as permanent preservation of every external retrieval state.

4.8 Reproducibility Boundary
Deterministic query construction means that equivalent LitSearch inputs produce equivalent source-specific request logic under the same application implementation and configuration.
It does not guarantee that an external literature database will return an identical record set indefinitely.

Retrieval outcomes may change because of:
• newly indexed publications
• metadata corrections
• record updates
• retractions
• changes in upstream indexing
• changes in source ranking behavior
• API availability or operational changes

LitSearch therefore distinguishes reproducibility of the retrieval procedure from permanent identity of externally maintained search results.
By making query construction, filtering, capability differences, and retrieval state explicit, LitSearch provides an auditable basis for reconstructing how a literature search was performed.

_______________________________________
5. MeSH-Based Refinement (PubMed and Europe PMC)
LitSearch supports controlled-vocabulary refinement using Medical Subject Headings (MeSH) for retrieval sources with compatible MeSH search functionality. In the current implementation, MeSH-based query refinement is supported for PubMed and Europe PMC.
MeSH refinement is treated as an explicit retrieval operation. Selected descriptors and their logical relationships become part of the search state rather than being applied as an undocumented modification of the result set.

5.1 MeSH Descriptors
Medical Subject Headings provide a controlled vocabulary for representing biomedical concepts.
Within LitSearch, MeSH descriptors may be used to supplement free-text retrieval with structured concept-based refinement.
Selected MeSH terms can be combined using:
• AND semantics for conjunctive narrowing
• OR semantics for disjunctive expansion

The selected logical mode determines the relationship among multiple MeSH terms. The resulting MeSH expression is then combined with the free-text query and other applicable filters.

5.2 PubMed MeSH Retrieval
For PubMed retrieval, LitSearch represents selected descriptors using the PubMed MeSH Terms field.
Conceptually, a selected descriptor is translated into an expression of the form:
"descriptor"[MeSH Terms]
When multiple descriptors are selected, LitSearch combines these expressions using the selected AND or OR mode.
For example, the logical structure may be represented as:
free-text query
AND
(MeSH term A OR MeSH term B)
or:
free-text query
AND
(MeSH term A AND MeSH term B)

Publication-year and abstract-availability filters may additionally be combined with this query.
This construction keeps the relationship between free-text retrieval, controlled-vocabulary refinement, and other filters explicit.

5.3 Europe PMC MeSH Retrieval
Europe PMC also supports MeSH-aware retrieval within the current LitSearch implementation.
Selected descriptors are translated into Europe PMC query expressions of the form:
MESH:"descriptor"
Multiple descriptors are combined using the selected AND or OR mode.
The resulting MeSH expression is subsequently combined with the free-text query and other applicable Europe PMC filters using AND.

Conceptually:
(free-text query)
AND
(MESH:"term A" OR MESH:"term B")
AND
additional filters
or, when conjunctive MeSH refinement is selected:
(free-text query)
AND
(MESH:"term A" AND MESH:"term B")
AND
additional filters

This allows PubMed and Europe PMC to participate in a common user-facing MeSH refinement workflow while retaining their respective source-specific query syntax.

5.4 MeSH Refinement and All Sources
MeSH refinement requires special interpretation in a federated retrieval workflow.
Within All Sources, MeSH parameters can be propagated to the participating sources that support compatible MeSH retrieval:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC

The other current primary retrieval sources do not provide equivalent LitSearch MeSH filtering:
• OpenAlex
• Semantic Scholar
• Crossref
• DOAJ

Consequently, applying MeSH to PubMed and Europe PMC does not constitute a MeSH filter over the complete federated result set.
For this reason, LitSearch does not expose MeSH as a full-result-set filtering capability for All Sources.
This distinction prevents partial source-specific filtering from being represented as if all records in a heterogeneous federated search had been evaluated against the same controlled-vocabulary constraint.

5.5 MeSH Retrieval versus MeSH Metadata Enrichment
LitSearch distinguishes MeSH-based retrieval from MeSH metadata enrichment.
MeSH-based retrieval changes the query submitted to a compatible literature source and can therefore influence which records are retrieved.
MeSH metadata enrichment operates after a record has already been retrieved. It may augment an existing canonical Paper record with MeSH descriptors without determining whether that record entered the search result set.
In the current enrichment workflow, the PubMed MeSH provider can obtain MeSH descriptor metadata for applicable records. PubMed records can use their PubMed identifier directly, while suitable non-PubMed records may be resolved through a DOI-to-PubMed identifier workflow before MeSH metadata are retrieved.
This enrichment process does not convert a non-PubMed source into a MeSH-searchable retrieval source and does not retroactively apply a MeSH filter to the search that produced the record.
The distinction can therefore be summarized as:
MeSH retrieval
→ influences record selection
MeSH enrichment
→ augments metadata of an already retrieved record

Maintaining this distinction is important for interpreting both retrieval provenance and enriched metadata correctly.

5.6 Transparency and Reproducibility
A central methodological objective of MeSH refinement is transparency.
The retrieval logic should make explicit:
• selected MeSH descriptors
• the AND or OR relationship among multiple descriptors
• the free-text query
• applicable additional filters
• the retrieval source to which the MeSH expression is applied

This makes it possible to document and inspect the controlled-vocabulary component of a search strategy separately from the source-specific translation used to execute it.
Reproducibility remains subject to the external literature source. Changes in MeSH indexing, bibliographic metadata, database content, or upstream search behavior may affect records returned at a later date even when LitSearch reconstructs the same query logic.
LitSearch therefore provides reproducibility of the MeSH query construction procedure rather than a guarantee of permanently identical external search results.

_______________________________________
6. Search Execution and Result Inspection
Following query construction, LitSearch executes retrieval requests against one or more literature sources using source-specific retrieval mechanisms. Although external databases differ in query semantics, pagination models, sorting capabilities, filtering behavior, metadata structures, and operational constraints, retrieved records are presented through a common inspection framework.
The current implementation supports source-specific retrieval from:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC
• OpenAlex
• Semantic Scholar
• Crossref
• DOAJ

In addition, the All Sources workflow performs federated retrieval across these six primary retrieval sources.

6.1 Source-Specific Search Execution
For a source-specific search, LitSearch translates the explicit search state into the retrieval operations supported by the selected source and retrieval mode.
A search may include:
• free-text retrieval
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• MeSH refinement where supported
• source-supported sorting
• pagination and navigation state

Not all combinations are available for every source. Search execution therefore follows the capabilities of the active connector and retrieval mode rather than assuming uniform behavior across literature APIs.
Retrieved source records are normalized into canonical Paper objects before presentation and downstream processing.

6.2 Pagination Models
LitSearch supports heterogeneous pagination mechanisms while presenting them through a common application interface.
Four pagination models are represented in the current architecture:
• page-based pagination
• cursor-based pagination
• token-based pagination
• snapshot-based pagination

Their use depends on the active source or retrieval mode.
PubMed, OpenAlex, Crossref, and DOAJ are represented through page-oriented navigation within LitSearch.
Europe PMC uses cursor-based retrieval. Cursor state is maintained as required to support sequential navigation and applicable deeper-page workflows.
Semantic Scholar relevance retrieval uses offset-based upstream retrieval represented through page-oriented navigation in LitSearch.
Semantic Scholar chronological bulk retrieval uses continuation tokens rather than numeric page offsets.
All Sources uses temporary snapshot-based pagination. After the federated candidate set has been processed, individual result pages can be derived from the same temporary ordered snapshot while that state remains available.
These pagination strategies are not interchangeable. Navigation controls are therefore determined by the capabilities of the active source and retrieval mode.

6.3 Sorting and Result Ordering
Sorting behavior is similarly source-dependent.
The current LitSearch capability model supports the following user-facing sort combinations:
• PubMed — Relevance; Most Recent
• Europe PMC — Relevance
• OpenAlex — Relevance; Most Recent; Oldest First
• Semantic Scholar relevance mode — Relevance
• Semantic Scholar bulk mode — Most Recent; Oldest First
• Crossref — Relevance; Most Recent; Oldest First
• DOAJ — Relevance
• All Sources — Relevance; Most Recent; Oldest First

A sort label should not be interpreted as evidence that every source implements ordering through the same upstream algorithm or metadata field.
Unsupported sort requests may be normalized to a supported mode where required. LitSearch avoids representing a semantically different source operation as if it were equivalent to the requested sort.

6.4 All Sources Search Execution
All Sources search execution differs from a source-specific search because multiple independent retrieval operations contribute to a single result set.
Conceptually, execution follows:
Source-specific candidate retrieval
↓
Canonical normalization
↓
Candidate aggregation
↓
Cross-source duplicate detection
↓
Metadata merging
↓
Deterministic result ordering
↓
Temporary result snapshot
↓
Page slicing and presentation

Candidate retrieval is performed concurrently where possible.
Each participating source is handled independently. If an individual source fails during candidate retrieval, the federated workflow can continue with records obtained from the remaining sources. Failed-source information is retained as part of the processed All Sources state.
All Sources therefore supports partial-result operation rather than requiring every external API to succeed before any federated results can be presented.

6.5 Result Inspection
Normalized records are presented through a common inspection framework.
Depending on available metadata, users may inspect:
• title
• authors
• journal or venue
• publication year and publication date
• abstract
• DOI
• PMCID where available
• source attribution
• MeSH descriptors where available
• subject concepts where available
• full-text availability indicators
• external source links

Individual records can additionally be opened through source-aware detail routes.
The canonical representation enables consistent presentation while preserving the identity of the originating literature source.

6.6 Paper Detail Processing and Enrichment
Detail-page processing is distinct from initial result-list retrieval.
When a paper detail page is requested, LitSearch first retrieves or resolves the corresponding source-specific record into a canonical Paper object.
The resulting record can subsequently pass through the configured metadata-enrichment pipeline before detail presentation.
In the current implementation, the detail workflow can apply:
• PubMed-derived MeSH metadata enrichment
• OpenCitations citation-count enrichment
• OpenCitations reference-count enrichment

Enrichment augments an existing record and does not add new papers to the result set.
Where enrichment modifies a supported metadata field, provider provenance can be retained through the canonical enrichment_sources mapping.
Failure of an enrichment provider does not invalidate the underlying retrieved paper.

6.7 Traceability and External Evaluation
A central methodological objective of result inspection is preservation of traceability.
Retrieved records retain source attribution and, where available, identifiers and external URLs that permit comparison with authoritative source records.
This enables users to verify bibliographic information against the originating literature database or DOI-linked destination rather than treating the normalized LitSearch representation as an independent bibliographic authority.
Metadata enrichment is likewise distinguishable from primary retrieval through field-level enrichment provenance where recorded.

6.8 Operational Variability
Search execution depends on external scholarly APIs.
Temporary conditions may include:
• API latency
• rate limiting
• timeouts
• transient service errors
• incomplete responses
• upstream metadata changes

Such conditions can affect an individual execution without implying a change in LitSearch retrieval methodology.
LitSearch uses source-specific error handling, retry behavior where implemented, temporary operational state, and partial-result handling for federated retrieval to reduce the operational impact of external variability.
Nevertheless, successful reconstruction of a search procedure does not guarantee permanent identity of the records returned by independently maintained external databases.

6.9 Methodological Role of Result Inspection
Search execution and result inspection are intended to support literature identification, exploratory searching, retrieval verification, and preliminary inspection.
They do not constitute:
• study eligibility assessment
• methodological quality appraisal
• risk-of-bias assessment
• evidence synthesis
• scientific interpretation of retrieved findings

These activities remain outside the methodological scope of LitSearch.
The result-inspection workflow therefore provides a transparent interface between reproducible computational retrieval and subsequent researcher-led review processes.

________________________________________
7. Canonical Data Model and Normalization
A fundamental architectural principle of LitSearch is the transformation of heterogeneous source metadata into a shared internal representation.
External literature databases differ in field names, identifier systems, author structures, date representations, subject metadata, abstract availability, and link formats. Direct downstream processing of these source-specific structures would therefore introduce substantial duplication and inconsistency.
LitSearch addresses this through a canonical Paper model.
All primary retrieval connectors transform retrieved source records into Paper objects before those records participate in shared downstream workflows.

7.1 Canonical Paper Model
The current canonical Paper representation contains the following fields:
• id
• source
• title
• authors
• journal
• year
• publication_date
• abstract
• doi
• pmcid
• url
• mesh_terms
• has_full_text
• concepts
• citation_count
• reference_count
• enrichment_sources

These fields form a shared internal schema rather than a requirement that every upstream literature source supply every metadata element.
Unavailable metadata remain empty or unset as appropriate.

7.2 Identity and Source Provenance
The id field represents the record identifier used by the corresponding LitSearch source integration.
The source field identifies the retrieval source associated with the normalized record.
Identifier availability varies between sources. Depending on the record, additional persistent or bibliographic identifiers may include:
• DOI
• PMCID

For PubMed records, the PubMed identifier can be represented through the canonical record id rather than through a separate dedicated pmid field.
This distinction is relevant when interpreting the internal data model: the canonical Paper model does not currently contain a separate pmid attribute.
During cross-source aggregation, source provenance can additionally be combined when duplicate records originating from multiple sources are merged.

7.3 Bibliographic Metadata
Core bibliographic fields include:
• title
• authors
• journal or venue
• publication year
• publication date
• abstract
• DOI
• URL

Source connectors translate their native response structures into these common fields.
Normalization does not imply that metadata supplied by different databases are necessarily identical. Sources may differ in:
• title formatting
• author representation
• journal naming
• date precision
• abstract availability
• identifier completeness
• URL selection

The canonical representation provides structural consistency while preserving the limitations of the underlying source metadata.

7.4 Subject and Full-Text Metadata
The canonical model additionally supports:
• mesh_terms
• concepts
• has_full_text

These fields are source-dependent.
MeSH descriptors are primarily associated with biomedical metadata and should not be interpreted as a universal subject classification across all retrieval sources.
The concepts field can contain source-dependent subject or concept metadata where available. Such concepts are not assumed to be semantically equivalent to MeSH.
The has_full_text field represents a source-derived indication of full-text availability where the corresponding connector can determine such information. It does not itself store full-text article content.

7.5 Enrichment Metadata
The canonical model supports metadata that may be added after primary retrieval.
Current enrichment-related fields include:
• citation_count
• reference_count
• enrichment_sources

OpenCitations can provide citation_count and reference_count for DOI-identified records when the corresponding metadata are available.
The enrichment_sources mapping records provider provenance for metadata fields modified through the enrichment pipeline.
This provenance mechanism is specific to enrichment. It should not be interpreted as a complete provenance history for every canonical field.

7.6 Metadata Enrichment and Merge Semantics
The enrichment pipeline operates on an existing canonical Paper record.
For scalar enrichment fields, LitSearch preserves an existing populated value and fills the field only when the current canonical value is empty.
Current scalar fields supported by the generic enrichment merge mechanism include:
• abstract
• citation_count
• reference_count

For supported list fields, enrichment values can be combined with existing values while avoiding case-insensitive duplicates and preserving deterministic ordering.
Current list fields supported by the enrichment merge mechanism include:
• mesh_terms
• concepts

Provider provenance is recorded for fields that are actually changed by enrichment.
An enrichment provider that does not match the record or does not supply usable metadata leaves the existing Paper record unchanged.

7.7 Cross-Source Record Merging
Cross-source deduplication uses a separate record-merging process from metadata enrichment.
When two retrieved records are determined to represent the same publication, LitSearch preserves populated metadata from the primary record and can fill missing values from the matched duplicate record.
Source information is combined so that the merged record retains provenance of the contributing retrieval sources.
This operation should be distinguished from enrichment:
Cross-source record merging
→ combines metadata from duplicate retrieval records
Metadata enrichment
→ augments an already retrieved record through a configured enrichment provider

Both operate on the canonical Paper representation, but they serve different methodological purposes.

7.8 Normalization and Deduplication
Normalization also supports deterministic duplicate detection.
Before identifier comparison, DOI values are normalized to reduce superficial formatting differences such as:
• letter case
• DOI resolver prefixes
• DOI labels
• selected trailing punctuation

Where persistent identifiers do not establish a match, selected bibliographic metadata can be normalized for comparison.
Current metadata-based duplicate detection can use:
• normalized title
• normalized first author
• publication year

Identifier conflicts are used as safeguards against inappropriate metadata-based merging.
A constrained tolerant comparison may additionally accommodate a one-year publication-year difference under specific identifier conditions.
Normalization therefore contributes both to structural interoperability and to deterministic cross-source duplicate detection.

7.9 Canonical Model versus Export Schema
The canonical Paper model is broader than the current export schema.
CSV and XLSX exports currently use a defined bibliographic subset consisting of:
• ID
• Source
• Title
• Authors
• Journal
• Year
• DOI
• PMCID
• URL

RIS exports similarly map a selected subset of normalized bibliographic fields into RIS tags.
Consequently, fields such as:
• abstract
• mesh_terms
• concepts
• citation_count
• reference_count
• enrichment_sources

are part of the canonical internal representation but are not currently exported as standard fields in all supported export formats.
This distinction is important because normalization into Paper does not imply automatic serialization of every canonical attribute into every output format.

7.10 Methodological Role of the Canonical Model
The canonical Paper model provides a stable interface between source-specific retrieval and shared application behavior.
Conceptually:
External source record
↓
Source-specific mapping
↓
Canonical Paper
↓
Shared LitSearch processing

Shared processing can include:
• presentation
• aggregation
• duplicate detection
• record merging
• sorting
• pagination state
• detail processing
• metadata enrichment
• export generation

This architecture reduces coupling between external API schemas and downstream application logic.
New retrieval sources can therefore be integrated by translating their records into the canonical representation without requiring a separate downstream processing architecture for each source.
The canonical model should be understood as an interoperability layer rather than an assertion that heterogeneous literature databases provide identical metadata quality, semantics, or completeness.

_______________________________________
8. Data Export and Reproducibility
LitSearch supports structured export of retrieved bibliographic records for downstream use in reference-management, review, analysis, and archival workflows.
The current implementation supports:
• CSV
• RIS
• XLSX

Export operates on normalized retrieval records, but the export schema is intentionally narrower than the complete canonical Paper model. Each format serializes a defined subset of available metadata.
This distinction is important for methodological interpretation: the presence of metadata in the internal Paper representation does not imply that the field is included in every export format.

8.1 Export Search Context
An export request is associated with explicit retrieval parameters.
Depending on the selected source and workflow, export state may include:
• free-text query
• source
• export format
• export scope
• sort mode
• requested result count
• page
• publication-year restrictions
• abstract-availability filtering
• MeSH terms
• MeSH logical mode
• source-specific retrieval mode
• pagination or snapshot state where applicable

These parameters allow export generation to remain linked to the retrieval context from which the exported records are derived.

Source-specific capability rules continue to apply during export. An unsupported sort or filter should not be interpreted as valid merely because it was supplied as an export parameter.

8.2 CSV Export
CSV export provides a tabular bibliographic representation suitable for spreadsheet software, scripting environments, and general data-processing workflows.
The current CSV schema contains:
• ID
• Source
• Title
• Authors
• Journal
• Year
• DOI
• PMCID
• URL

Authors are serialized from the normalized author representation into the tabular export structure.
The CSV export does not currently include every canonical Paper field.

In particular, the standard CSV schema does not currently export:
• abstract
• publication_date
• mesh_terms
• concepts
• has_full_text
• citation_count
• reference_count
• enrichment_sources

8.3 XLSX Export
XLSX export provides a spreadsheet-oriented representation of retrieved bibliographic records.
The current XLSX export uses the same principal nine-field bibliographic schema as CSV:
• ID
• Source
• Title
• Authors
• Journal
• Year
• DOI
• PMCID
• URL

The spreadsheet format provides a structured tabular file but does not currently imply a richer metadata export than CSV.
Canonical metadata fields outside this selected schema are not automatically included solely because they exist in the internal Paper model.

8.4 RIS Export
RIS export provides a bibliographic interchange format suitable for import into compatible reference-management workflows.
LitSearch maps normalized Paper metadata into selected RIS tags.

The current mapping includes:
• TY — record type, represented as JOUR
• TI — title
• AU — author, repeated for individual authors
• JO — journal
• PY — publication year
• DO — DOI
• AN — PMCID where available
• UR — URL
• ID — LitSearch/source record identifier
• ER — end of record

RIS export therefore represents a selected bibliographic subset of the canonical record.
Fields such as abstracts, MeSH terms, concepts, citation counts, reference counts, and enrichment provenance are not currently part of the standard LitSearch RIS mapping.

8.5 Export Scope and Retrieval Processing
Export generation is connected to the LitSearch retrieval architecture rather than functioning as an unrelated metadata download mechanism.
Source-specific exports use the corresponding source retrieval logic and applicable search parameters.

For All Sources, export generation can use the federated retrieval workflow and its centralized processing, including:
• source-specific candidate retrieval
• canonical normalization
• cross-source deduplication
• metadata merging
• deterministic ordering

Where a compatible All Sources snapshot is available and applicable to the workflow, snapshot state can contribute to stable retrieval continuity.
The exact records exported remain dependent on the selected export scope, source behavior, available retrieval state, and external API responses.

8.6 Asynchronous Export Processing
Potentially larger export operations can be executed asynchronously.
LitSearch uses ARQ with Redis-backed task infrastructure for background export processing.

Conceptually:
Export request
↓
Export job creation
↓
Background retrieval and processing
↓
File generation
↓
Completed export result
This separates longer-running export work from the immediate HTTP request-response cycle.
Job state can be tracked through temporary application infrastructure while the export is being processed.
This infrastructure should not be interpreted as permanent archival storage of either the search or the generated export.

8.7 Export Reproducibility
LitSearch aims to make the procedure used to generate an export explicit and reconstructable.
Procedural reproducibility depends on preserving or documenting relevant retrieval parameters such as:
• query
• source
• filters
• sort mode
• retrieval mode
• export scope
• controlled-vocabulary parameters where applicable

However, repeating the same export procedure at a later date does not guarantee a byte-identical file or an identical set of publications.
External literature databases may change because of:
• newly indexed records
• corrected metadata
• updated abstracts
• changed identifiers
• retractions
• source-side ranking changes
• API behavior changes

Temporary cursor, token, cache, or snapshot state may also expire.
LitSearch therefore distinguishes reproducibility of the export procedure from permanent preservation of an external database state.

8.8 Search and Export Consistency
Search and export are designed to share source-specific retrieval and normalization logic wherever applicable.
This reduces the risk that the same documented query is interpreted through entirely separate implementations for interactive retrieval and export.
Nevertheless, interactive result presentation and export generation are not assumed to be identical operations in every respect.
Differences may arise from:
• export scope
• requested result count
• pagination state
• asynchronous execution
• temporary snapshot availability
• source-specific result-window constraints
• changes in an external API between executions

Consistency should therefore be interpreted as reuse of common retrieval semantics and processing components, not as an unconditional guarantee that every displayed page and every subsequently generated export will always contain an identical record set.

8.9 Export Provenance Boundaries
The Source field preserves retrieval-source attribution in CSV and XLSX exports.
For records produced through cross-source deduplication, source information may contain combined provenance from contributing retrieval sources.
This source attribution should be distinguished from enrichment provenance.
The canonical enrichment_sources field can identify providers responsible for enrichment changes, but this field is not currently included in the standard CSV, XLSX, or RIS export schemas.
Consequently, the current export formats preserve retrieval-source information more directly than field-level enrichment provenance.

8.10 Current Export Boundaries
The current export layer is intentionally focused on core bibliographic interoperability.
It does not yet provide a configurable export schema for arbitrary canonical Paper fields.
Metadata present internally but absent from the current standard export schemas can include:
• abstracts
• publication dates beyond the exported year
• MeSH descriptors
• concepts
• full-text indicators
• citation counts
• reference counts
• enrichment provenance

Additional formats or configurable field selection may be introduced in future versions, but such planned functionality should be distinguished from the currently implemented export behavior.
The methodological principle remains that exported content, export parameters, and retrieval provenance should be explicit rather than inferred.

_______________________________________
9. Validation Strategy
LitSearch uses layered validation to evaluate retrieval behavior, normalization, filtering, pagination, aggregation, deduplication, export generation, enrichment, and user-facing search behavior.
Because LitSearch depends on independently maintained external scholarly APIs, validation distinguishes deterministic application logic from behavior that depends on live external services.
The validation strategy combines:
• automated unit and regression tests
• connector and workflow tests
• Docker-dependent integration tests
• live-source sanity checks
• manual application-level inspection
• release-level validation

These validation layers serve different purposes and should not be interpreted as interchangeable forms of evidence.

9.1 Automated Unit and Regression Tests
The primary automated test suite is implemented with pytest.
Unit and regression tests are used to validate deterministic application behavior such as:
• query construction
• source capability definitions
• sort normalization
• filter propagation
• pagination logic
• canonical metadata mapping
• deduplication
• metadata merging
• enrichment merging
• cache and request-state behavior
• export formatting
• user-interface conditions
• regression fixes

Regression tests are added when a specific defect or semantic inconsistency is identified.
A typical development workflow is:
Observed or anticipated failure
↓
Regression test demonstrating the expected behavior
↓
Minimal implementation change
↓
Targeted test execution
↓
Broader non-integration test execution

This test-first approach reduces the risk that a local correction silently changes unrelated retrieval behavior.

9.2 Capability Validation
LitSearch maintains explicit source- and mode-specific search capabilities.
Automated tests validate capability properties such as:
• pagination type
• supported sort modes
• previous-page support
• last-page support
• direct page-jump support
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• MeSH filtering
• configured result-window metadata where applicable

Capability validation is important because these definitions influence both application behavior and the retrieval options presented to users.
Tests additionally verify selected relationships between capability declarations and user-interface behavior.
A capability declaration is therefore treated as part of the methodological contract of a connector rather than merely as presentation metadata.

9.3 Connector and Workflow Validation
Connector-focused tests validate translation between LitSearch retrieval parameters and source-specific behavior.
Depending on the connector, validation may cover:
• free-text query construction
• year filtering
• abstract filtering
• MeSH construction where supported
• sort translation
• pagination parameters
• response normalization
• detail retrieval
• error handling

Workflow tests can additionally verify interactions between components, including:
• All Sources retrieval
• snapshot reuse
• deduplication
• metadata merging
• export generation
• detail-page enrichment

These tests are used to verify application behavior across component boundaries without assuming that every test necessarily contacts a live external API.

9.4 Docker-Dependent Integration Tests
The pytest configuration defines an integration marker for tests that require running Docker services.
These tests are conceptually distinct from tests whose filenames happen to contain the word integration.
A test is treated as Docker-dependent integration testing according to its pytest marker and runtime requirements rather than solely according to its filename.
Such tests may depend on infrastructure including:
• Redis
• the LitSearch application container
• background worker infrastructure

Separating these tests from the default deterministic suite allows local application logic to be tested independently from running service dependencies.

9.5 Live-Source Sanity Checks
LitSearch additionally uses sanity checks against running application workflows and external scholarly APIs.
These checks can be used to verify that configured connectors still function against current upstream services.
Examples include:
• executing representative searches
• verifying that results are returned
• checking pagination behavior
• opening paper-detail routes
• generating exports
• verifying asynchronous export completion
• testing selected source-specific filters
• exercising All Sources behavior

Live-source sanity checks are particularly important because a connector can remain internally correct while an external API changes its operational behavior.
However, live-source checks are inherently less deterministic than isolated automated tests.

9.6 External API Variability
A failed live-source check does not by itself establish a LitSearch software defect.
External services can temporarily return:
• HTTP 429 rate-limit responses
• HTTP 5xx service errors
• timeouts
• empty responses
• transient authentication failures
• delayed or incomplete metadata

When a live check fails, the failure should therefore be investigated before modifying connector logic.
Useful diagnostic steps can include:
• rerunning the affected test
• calling the connector directly
• comparing authenticated and unauthenticated behavior where appropriate
• inspecting the raw HTTP response
• checking whether other connectors remain functional
• determining whether the failure is reproducible

Code changes should be based on reproducible implementation evidence rather than a single transient external failure.

9.7 Federated Retrieval Validation
All Sources requires validation beyond individual connector correctness.
Relevant behaviors include:
• candidate retrieval from participating sources
• partial-source failure handling
• canonical normalization
• duplicate detection
• identifier-conflict safeguards
• metadata merging
• deterministic relevance interleaving
• chronological ordering
• snapshot creation
• snapshot reuse
• request-signature compatibility
• stable page slicing from a compatible snapshot

Federated validation should also account for the fact that filters are not necessarily implemented identically across all participating sources.
A successful All Sources test therefore does not imply that every source applies every filter through the same upstream mechanism.

9.8 Deduplication Validation
Cross-source deduplication is validated independently because inappropriate merging can alter the apparent composition of a literature set.
Tests can verify:
• normalized DOI matching
• PMID-based identity where applicable
• PMCID-based identity where applicable
• normalized title comparison
• first-author comparison
• publication-year comparison
• identifier-conflict protection
• constrained one-year tolerance
• metadata preservation during merging
• filling of missing metadata
• combined source provenance

Both false-negative and false-positive duplicate behavior are methodologically relevant.
The objective is deterministic and inspectable record consolidation rather than probabilistic similarity scoring.

9.9 Export Validation
Export validation covers both file generation and retrieval semantics.
Tests can verify:
• CSV generation
• XLSX generation
• RIS generation
• expected field mappings
• source attribution
• search-parameter propagation
• sort normalization
• filter handling
• All Sources export behavior
• asynchronous job processing where applicable

Validation should distinguish format correctness from search-result identity.
A correctly formatted export can still differ between executions if the underlying external database changes.

9.10 Enrichment Validation
Metadata enrichment is tested separately from primary retrieval.
Validation can include:
• provider matching
• successful metadata retrieval
• scalar merge behavior
• list merge behavior
• preservation of existing populated values
• enrichment provenance
• provider failure isolation

The current enrichment architecture is designed so that failure of an enrichment provider does not invalidate the underlying retrieved Paper.
This property is methodologically important because optional metadata augmentation should not determine whether a primary retrieval result remains available.

9.11 Manual Application Validation
Automated tests are complemented by manual inspection of the running application.
Manual validation may include:
• entering representative search queries
• switching between sources
• applying supported filters
• inspecting sort options
• navigating result pages
• opening detail pages
• checking source links
• reviewing All Sources results
• downloading generated exports
• inspecting user-facing warnings

This layer is useful for detecting integration or presentation problems that may not be evident from isolated function-level tests.
Manual validation supplements rather than replaces automated regression coverage.

9.12 Release-Level Validation
Feature-level tests and successful connector checks do not automatically constitute complete release validation.
Before a release is considered validated, the intended release scope should be reviewed systematically across relevant workflows.
For the current v0.6.0 development line, implemented functionality includes substantial additions such as:
• Crossref retrieval integration
• DOAJ retrieval integration
• connector capability modeling
• expanded All Sources aggregation
• improved cross-source deduplication and metadata merging
• Europe PMC MeSH refinement
• metadata enrichment infrastructure
• PubMed-derived MeSH enrichment
• OpenCitations citation and reference counts

Individual components have been exercised through automated tests and development sanity checks.
Release-level validation should nevertheless be treated as a separate activity until the complete intended v0.6.0 validation checklist has been executed and documented.
The methods paper should therefore distinguish:
Implemented
≠
Individually tested
≠
Release validated

These states provide different levels of evidence about software readiness.

9.13 Continuous Validation Principle
LitSearch treats validation as an ongoing engineering process rather than a one-time release activity.
Whenever retrieval logic, source capabilities, normalization, aggregation, deduplication, enrichment, pagination, or export behavior changes, relevant tests should be updated or extended.
The preferred maintenance cycle is:
Implementation change
↓
Targeted regression coverage
↓
Broader automated testing
↓
Live or manual sanity checking where appropriate
↓
Version-controlled review
↓
Release-level validation when preparing a release

This layered approach supports methodological traceability while acknowledging that external scholarly retrieval systems remain operationally dynamic.

______________________________________
10. Ethical and Responsible Use

LitSearch is designed to support transparent and reproducible scientific literature retrieval.

It assists users in identifying, retrieving, organizing, inspecting, deduplicating, and exporting bibliographic records from external scholarly information sources.

The system does not determine the scientific validity, methodological quality, clinical relevance, or evidentiary strength of retrieved publications.

Human scholarly judgment remains necessary throughout the subsequent review process.

10.1 Retrieval Support, Not Evidence Synthesis
LitSearch operates primarily at the literature-identification and retrieval stage.
Its functions include:
• query construction
• source-specific literature retrieval
• controlled-vocabulary refinement where supported
• filtering
• sorting
• pagination
• federated retrieval
• metadata normalization
• cross-source deduplication
• metadata merging
• bibliographic export
• metadata enrichment

These operations should not be interpreted as:
• study screening
• eligibility assessment
• methodological quality appraisal
• risk-of-bias assessment
• evidence grading
• statistical synthesis
• interpretation of scientific findings
• clinical decision support

Retrieval identifies potentially relevant records. It does not establish whether those records should be included in a review or how their findings should be interpreted.

10.2 Human Oversight
Researchers remain responsible for the design, interpretation, and documentation of their literature-search strategy.
Human oversight is particularly important when:
• selecting databases
• constructing free-text queries
• selecting MeSH descriptors
• choosing AND/OR relationships
• defining publication-year restrictions
• interpreting differences between sources
• evaluating retrieved records
• deciding inclusion and exclusion criteria
• assessing study quality
• interpreting evidence

LitSearch makes retrieval operations more explicit, but it does not replace subject expertise, information-retrieval expertise, or review methodology.

10.3 Source Coverage and Retrieval Bias
No individual literature database provides complete coverage of scientific publishing.
The six primary LitSearch retrieval sources differ in:
• disciplinary scope
• indexing policies
• metadata completeness
• controlled-vocabulary support
• document coverage
• update frequency
• ranking behavior
• API functionality

Consequently, the records returned by LitSearch depend partly on the selected retrieval sources.
Using All Sources can broaden retrieval across multiple databases, but federated retrieval should not be interpreted as exhaustive coverage of the scientific literature.
The absence of a publication from a LitSearch result set does not establish that the publication does not exist or is irrelevant.

10.4 Federated Retrieval and Source Balance
All Sources combines bounded candidate sets from heterogeneous literature sources.
This architecture improves multi-source discovery but introduces methodological boundaries.
The federated result set depends on:
• which sources participate
• the candidate depth requested from each source
• source-specific ranking
• source-specific filter behavior
• source availability at execution time
• cross-source deduplication
• centralized result ordering

The relevance-oriented All Sources workflow uses deterministic source interleaving rather than a universal cross-source relevance score.
This mechanism should therefore be interpreted as source-balanced deterministic presentation, not as an objective ranking of scientific importance or relevance across databases.

10.5 Deduplication and Metadata Merging
Cross-source deduplication reduces repeated representations of the same publication.
However, duplicate detection is a bibliographic processing operation rather than a scientific assessment.
LitSearch uses persistent identifiers where available and constrained metadata comparisons where necessary.
Although safeguards reduce inappropriate merging, bibliographic records can contain incomplete, inconsistent, or erroneous metadata.
Users conducting high-stakes or formal reviews should therefore remain able to inspect identifiers and source records when duplicate resolution is methodologically important.
Metadata merging likewise combines complementary bibliographic information from records identified as duplicates. It does not determine which source is scientifically more authoritative or which publication is methodologically stronger.

10.6 Metadata Enrichment
Metadata enrichment is supplementary to primary retrieval.
Current enrichment workflows can add metadata such as:
• MeSH descriptors
• citation counts
• reference counts
Enrichment does not determine whether a record is retrieved and should not be interpreted as independent scientific validation of that record.
Provider-derived metadata may be incomplete, unavailable, delayed, or inconsistent with metadata from other services.
Where supported, LitSearch records field-level enrichment provenance so that metadata added through enrichment can be distinguished from the underlying retrieval record.

10.7 Responsible Interpretation of Citation Metadata
Citation and reference counts supplied through OpenCitations are bibliometric metadata.
They should not be interpreted as direct measures of:
• scientific quality
• methodological rigor
• evidentiary strength
• clinical relevance
• truth
• research impact in every context

Citation counts can be influenced by publication age, discipline, citation practices, database coverage, document type, visibility, and other factors unrelated to methodological quality.
A highly cited publication is therefore not necessarily a high-quality study, and a publication with few citations is not necessarily weak or irrelevant.
LitSearch exposes such counts as descriptive metadata only.

10.8 Controlled Vocabulary and Concept Metadata
MeSH descriptors, source concepts, keywords, and other subject metadata support retrieval and record description.
These metadata should not be treated as interchangeable classification systems.

In particular:
• MeSH is a controlled biomedical vocabulary
• source-specific concepts may use different taxonomies
• author keywords may reflect terminology chosen by publication authors
• automatically supplied subject metadata may follow source-specific indexing procedures

LitSearch therefore preserves the distinction between MeSH-based retrieval and other concept metadata rather than treating all subject terms as semantically equivalent.

10.9 External Links and Full-Text Availability
LitSearch may provide links to external records, DOI destinations, or available full-text resources.
The presence of an external or full-text link does not imply that LitSearch:
• hosts the publication
• owns the publication
• guarantees continued access
• verifies licensing conditions
• verifies the scientific content of the linked resource

Users remain responsible for complying with applicable access conditions, copyright requirements, institutional policies, and publisher terms.

10.10 Transparency of Limitations
Responsible use requires that limitations remain visible rather than being hidden behind a uniform interface.
LitSearch therefore distinguishes, where applicable:
• supported and unsupported filters
• source-specific sort behavior
• pagination differences
• controlled-vocabulary availability
• federated versus source-specific retrieval
• retrieval provenance
• enrichment provenance
• external API failures

A common interface should not be interpreted as evidence that the underlying literature databases have identical capabilities or semantics.

10.11 Reproducibility and Temporal Change
LitSearch supports procedural reproducibility by making retrieval parameters and processing steps explicit.
However, scientific databases are dynamic information systems.
A search repeated at a later date may produce different results because of:
• newly published or newly indexed records
• metadata corrections
• retractions
• updated indexing
• changed ranking algorithms
• source-side API changes
• temporary source availability

Responsible reporting should therefore document not only the query strategy but also relevant contextual information such as the literature sources used and the date or period in which retrieval was performed.
Procedural reproducibility should not be represented as permanent preservation of an external database state.

10.12 Privacy and Data Handling
LitSearch primarily processes bibliographic metadata and retrieval parameters.
Temporary application infrastructure such as Redis may store operational state required for:
• caching
• pagination
• All Sources snapshots
• background export jobs
• related retrieval workflows

Such temporary state supports application operation and should not be interpreted as a permanent scholarly archive.
Researchers should separately preserve search strategies, exported result sets, and other materials required by their own reproducibility or institutional policies.

10.13 Appropriate Methodological Role
LitSearch is most appropriately regarded as a literature-retrieval and bibliographic-processing instrument.
It can support researchers by making retrieval operations more explicit, repeatable, inspectable, and exportable.
It does not replace:
• librarians or information specialists
• domain experts
• systematic-review methodology
• critical appraisal
• statistical expertise
• scientific judgment

The methodological objective is therefore not automated scientific decision-making, but transparent computational support for researcher-controlled literature identification and retrieval.

________________________________________
11. Limitations
LitSearch is designed to improve the transparency, reproducibility, and interoperability of scientific literature retrieval. Its methodological and technical boundaries should nevertheless be considered when interpreting search results, exports, and enriched metadata.
The principal limitations arise from:
• dependence on external scholarly APIs
• incomplete and heterogeneous source coverage
• source-specific retrieval semantics
• bounded federated candidate retrieval
• imperfect bibliographic metadata
• deterministic but non-infallible deduplication
• temporary application state
• limited export schemas
• optional and incomplete metadata enrichment
• temporal changes in external databases

These limitations do not invalidate the retrieval methodology, but they define the conditions under which LitSearch results should be interpreted.

11.1 Dependence on External APIs
LitSearch retrieves bibliographic information from independently maintained external services.
The current primary retrieval sources are:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC
• OpenAlex
• Semantic Scholar
• Crossref
• DOAJ

Availability and behavior of these services are outside direct LitSearch control.
External APIs may introduce:
• rate limits
• authentication requirements
• temporary outages
• HTTP 429 responses
• HTTP 5xx responses
• timeouts
• changed endpoints
• changed response schemas
• changed ranking behavior
• changed pagination behavior
• changed API policies

Consequently, an otherwise valid LitSearch workflow can be temporarily degraded by an upstream service.

11.2 Incomplete Literature Coverage
The set of sources integrated into LitSearch does not constitute the complete scholarly literature.
Different databases index different publications, disciplines, document types, publishers, repositories, and time periods.
Even when All Sources is used, the resulting literature set should not be interpreted as exhaustive.
Relevant publications may remain absent because they are:
• not indexed by an integrated source
• not exposed through the corresponding API
• outside the source's coverage
• insufficiently described by available metadata
• not retrieved within the candidate depth used by the workflow

Additional scholarly sources may be integrated in future versions, but no finite set of retrieval APIs can be assumed to provide universal coverage.

11.3 Heterogeneous Retrieval Semantics
LitSearch provides a common interface over heterogeneous literature APIs, but the underlying retrieval semantics remain source-specific.
Sources differ in:
• free-text interpretation
• field indexing
• publication-date definitions
• relevance ranking
• abstract filtering
• controlled-vocabulary support
• sort capabilities
• pagination
• result-window constraints

A common LitSearch parameter should therefore not automatically be interpreted as an identical upstream operation across all sources.
For example, an abstract-availability filter may be implemented natively by one source and locally after retrieval for another.
Similarly, publication-year filtering may use different source-specific date representations.
The common interface normalizes interaction, not the internal search algorithms of external databases.

11.4 Controlled-Vocabulary Limitations
MeSH-based retrieval is currently supported for PubMed and Europe PMC.
OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Crossref, and DOAJ do not provide equivalent LitSearch MeSH filtering.
Consequently, MeSH cannot be applied as a semantically uniform constraint across the complete All Sources result set.
Although All Sources can propagate MeSH parameters to PubMed and Europe PMC, this represents partial source-specific filtering rather than full federated MeSH filtering.
For this reason, MeSH is not exposed as a complete All Sources filtering capability.
Source-specific concepts, keywords, and subject classifications should not be interpreted as interchangeable substitutes for MeSH.

11.5 All Sources Uses Bounded Candidate Retrieval
All Sources is a federated candidate-aggregation workflow rather than an exhaustive union of every record available from every participating database.
For each search, LitSearch retrieves a bounded candidate set from the participating sources before performing centralized processing.
The final federated result set is therefore influenced by:
• candidate depth
• upstream ranking
• source-specific result-window behavior
• source availability
• active filters
• duplicate removal

A potentially relevant publication that falls outside the retrieved candidate set of a source cannot subsequently enter the aggregated result set through that source.
All Sources should therefore be interpreted as reproducible bounded federated retrieval, not as exhaustive harvesting of all records matching a query in every database.

11.6 Partial Filter Semantics in All Sources
Filters cannot always be applied uniformly across every source participating in All Sources.
Publication-year and abstract filtering are supported across much of the retrieval architecture, but their exact implementation differs between connectors.
In the current All Sources implementation, Semantic Scholar does not receive the same year and abstract filter propagation used for several of the other participating connectors.
MeSH filtering is propagated only to PubMed and Europe PMC.
Consequently, a federated result set may contain records retrieved under different source-specific filtering semantics.
This limitation is important when interpreting All Sources as a methodological retrieval workflow. A common user-facing search state does not necessarily imply identical upstream constraints for every participating source.

11.7 Relevance Ordering across Sources
External literature databases use different and generally incomparable relevance-ranking systems.
LitSearch therefore does not calculate a universal normalized relevance score for All Sources.
For relevance-oriented federated retrieval, records retain their source-specific candidate ordering and are combined through deterministic round-robin interleaving in a fixed source order.
This improves deterministic and source-balanced presentation but does not establish a global relevance ranking.
The position of a record in an All Sources relevance result should therefore not be interpreted as a quantitative measure of relevance across databases.

11.8 Deduplication Limitations
Cross-source deduplication is deterministic but cannot guarantee perfect identification of every duplicate publication.
LitSearch prioritizes normalized persistent identifiers where available:
• DOI
• PMID
• PMCID

When identifier matching is insufficient, selected normalized metadata can be used, including:
• title
• first author
• publication year

A constrained tolerant comparison can accommodate a one-year publication-year difference under additional identifier safeguards.
Conflicting persistent identifiers prevent metadata-based merging.
Despite these safeguards, false negatives remain possible when duplicate records contain insufficient or substantially inconsistent metadata.
False positives also remain theoretically possible when distinct publications share highly similar bibliographic metadata.
Deduplicated records should therefore be regarded as algorithmically consolidated bibliographic records rather than independently verified publication identities.

11.9 Metadata Quality and Completeness
The quality of the canonical Paper representation depends on metadata supplied by external sources.
Records may contain:
• missing abstracts
• incomplete author lists
• missing or malformed identifiers
• inconsistent journal names
• incomplete publication dates
• differing publication years
• inconsistent URLs
• incomplete subject metadata

Normalization provides a consistent data structure but cannot reconstruct metadata that are unavailable or determine with certainty which conflicting external value is authoritative.
Cross-source metadata merging can fill some missing fields, but it does not eliminate upstream metadata-quality limitations.

11.10 Temporary Snapshot State
All Sources uses temporary Redis-backed snapshots to support stable pagination over a processed federated result set.
A snapshot can preserve:
• ordered normalized records
• duplicate-removal information
• per-source counts
• failed-source information
• retrieval request signature

Snapshots are temporary.
The default snapshot lifetime is approximately 900 seconds unless configured otherwise.
After expiration, an equivalent search may require fresh retrieval from external sources. If upstream databases have changed in the meantime, the newly constructed result set may differ from the expired snapshot.
Snapshots should therefore be interpreted as short-lived retrieval state rather than permanent archival representations of a literature search.

11.11 Cursor and Token State
Some source workflows depend on continuation state supplied by external APIs.
Europe PMC uses cursor-based retrieval, while Semantic Scholar bulk retrieval uses continuation tokens.
Such state may be:
• temporary
• source-dependent
• unsuitable for arbitrary direct page reconstruction
• invalidated by upstream changes

Consequently, not every pagination workflow can be reconstructed indefinitely from a numeric page value alone.
LitSearch abstracts these differences where possible, but it cannot convert every upstream pagination model into permanently stable random-access navigation.

11.12 Partial Source Failure
All Sources is designed to continue when an individual source retrieval fails.
This increases operational resilience, but a partially successful federated search is not equivalent to a search in which all six sources responded successfully.
If a source fails, its candidate records are absent from that execution.
Failed-source information can be retained in the All Sources processing state, but users should account for such failures when interpreting or documenting a federated result set.
Operational continuity should therefore not be confused with complete source participation.

11.13 Semantic Scholar Retrieval Constraints
Semantic Scholar exposes different behavior for relevance-oriented and chronological bulk retrieval.
In the current LitSearch integration:
• relevance mode uses page-oriented interaction backed by offset retrieval
• relevance mode exposes relevance sorting
• publication-year restrictions in relevance mode are not treated as a full-result-set filtering capability
• selected filtering can occur locally after upstream retrieval
• bulk mode uses continuation-token pagination
• bulk mode supports chronological ordering
• publication-year filtering in bulk mode can be transmitted upstream
• abstract filtering can involve local post-retrieval processing

Semantic Scholar is additionally subject to upstream rate limiting and operational availability.
These mode-specific differences mean that Semantic Scholar retrieval should not be interpreted as a single uniform search mechanism.

11.14 Metadata Enrichment Limitations
Metadata enrichment is optional and dependent on provider-specific identifiers and service availability.
Current enrichment includes PubMed-derived MeSH metadata and OpenCitations citation/reference counts for applicable records.
Enrichment may fail or remain unavailable because:
• a required identifier is missing
• DOI-to-PubMed resolution is unsuccessful
• the enrichment provider does not contain the record
• a metadata field is unavailable
• the external enrichment service is temporarily unavailable

Enrichment failure does not remove the underlying retrieved Paper.
The current enrichment workflow is primarily associated with individual paper-detail processing and should not be interpreted as a mandatory enrichment stage for all search-result lists or exports.

11.15 Citation and Reference Count Limitations
Citation and reference counts obtained through OpenCitations depend on DOI identification and the coverage of the external OpenCitations service.
Counts may therefore be unavailable for some records.
They may also differ from counts reported by other bibliometric databases because services can differ in:
• indexed sources
• citation-link coverage
• update timing
• identifier matching
• record correction

LitSearch does not reconcile citation counts across multiple bibliometric providers in the current implementation.
Citation and reference counts should therefore be interpreted as provider-derived metadata rather than universal bibliometric totals.

11.16 Export Limitations
The current export schemas contain a selected subset of the canonical Paper model.
CSV and XLSX currently export:
• ID
• Source
• Title
• Authors
• Journal
• Year
• DOI
• PMCID
• URL

RIS exports a corresponding selected bibliographic representation.
Canonical fields such as abstracts, MeSH descriptors, concepts, citation counts, reference counts, full-text indicators, and enrichment provenance are not currently included as standard fields across these exports.
Exporting a result set therefore does not preserve every metadata element available internally in LitSearch.
Configurable field selection and additional export formats may be considered in future versions.

11.17 Temporary Storage and Archival Boundaries
Redis is used for operational state such as pagination support, temporary snapshots, and background-task coordination.
This infrastructure is not intended to function as a permanent research archive.
Researchers requiring long-term reproducibility should preserve appropriate research artifacts independently, which may include:
• documented search strategies
• retrieval dates
• exported record sets
• source selections
• filter settings
• software version information

Temporary application state should not be relied upon as the sole record of a completed literature search.

11.18 Temporal Reproducibility
LitSearch aims for reproducibility of computational retrieval procedures.
It cannot guarantee permanent identity of externally retrieved result sets.

Repeated execution may produce different records because of:
• newly indexed publications
• metadata corrections
• retractions
• database updates
• changed ranking behavior
• API changes
• source availability
• changed enrichment coverage

The strongest reproducibility claim supported by LitSearch is therefore procedural:
given an identified LitSearch implementation, configuration, query, source selection, filters, and retrieval mode, the system defines how the corresponding external retrieval operation is constructed and processed.
This should be distinguished from permanent preservation of the external information environment in which the original search was executed.

11.19 Scope Limitations
LitSearch is a retrieval and bibliographic-processing application.
It does not currently perform:
• automated study screening
• eligibility decisions
• risk-of-bias assessment
• evidence grading
• statistical meta-analysis
• automated evidence synthesis
• scientific interpretation of article findings

These activities require methodological and domain-specific judgment beyond the intended scope of the application.
The system should therefore be used as one component of a broader researcher-controlled literature-review workflow rather than as an autonomous review system.

________________________________________
12. Living Methods and Versioning Policy
LitSearch is developed as an evolving scientific software system.
The accompanying methods documentation is therefore maintained as a Living Methods Paper: a methodological description that can be revised when the implementation changes while preserving a stable account of the system's core design principles.
The purpose of this approach is to avoid two undesirable extremes:
• documentation that becomes obsolete as the software evolves
• documentation that changes so frequently that the underlying methodology becomes difficult to identify

LitSearch therefore distinguishes stable methodological principles from implementation-dependent details.

12.1 Stable Methodological Core
The following principles are intended to remain stable across LitSearch versions:
• explicit and parameterized literature retrieval
• source-specific translation of retrieval parameters
• normalization into a canonical Paper representation
• transparent source provenance
• explicit source and mode capabilities
• deterministic processing where under application control
• cross-source deduplication using inspectable rules
• separation of retrieval from metadata enrichment
• transparent export of bibliographic records
• researcher-controlled interpretation of retrieved literature
• documentation of methodological limitations

These principles define the conceptual architecture of LitSearch independently of individual external APIs.
A future connector, enrichment provider, export format, or interface change should therefore integrate with these principles rather than requiring the methodological foundation to be redefined.

12.2 Implementation-Dependent Components
Other aspects of LitSearch are expected to evolve over time.
These can include:
• integrated retrieval sources
• API endpoints
• authentication requirements
• rate-limit handling
• query syntax
• pagination mechanisms
• supported sort modes
• source-specific filters
• result-window constraints
• canonical metadata fields
• deduplication safeguards
• enrichment providers
• enrichment fields
• export formats
• export schemas
• caching strategies
• background-processing infrastructure
• user-interface controls

Changes to these components should be documented when they materially alter how a literature search is executed, processed, interpreted, or exported.

12.3 Source Capability Versioning
LitSearch models retrieval capabilities explicitly at source and, where necessary, retrieval-mode level.
Capabilities can include:
• pagination type
• supported sort modes
• previous-page navigation
• last-page navigation
• direct page jumping
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• MeSH filtering
• result-window metadata where applicable

These capability declarations form part of the implemented retrieval behavior.
If an upstream API changes or a connector gains or loses functionality, the corresponding capability definition and its validation tests should be updated together.
This prevents the user interface and methodological documentation from relying on obsolete assumptions about a source.

12.4 Retrieval Source Evolution
A new retrieval source should be integrated through the connector architecture rather than by embedding source-specific behavior throughout shared application code.
Where applicable, a new connector should define:
• query construction
• filtering behavior
• sorting behavior
• pagination behavior
• metadata normalization
• detail retrieval
• operational error handling
• source capabilities

Participation in All Sources is an additional integration decision.
A source should only participate in federated retrieval when its connector behavior and normalized records can be incorporated appropriately into the aggregation workflow.
Adding a source to LitSearch does not therefore imply that it automatically becomes part of every multi-source workflow.

12.5 Enrichment Provider Evolution
Metadata enrichment is versioned conceptually separately from primary retrieval.
A new enrichment provider may augment already retrieved canonical records without becoming a literature retrieval source.
An enrichment provider should define, where applicable:
• record matching requirements
• identifier requirements
• metadata fields supplied
• merge behavior
• provenance
• error isolation

The addition or removal of an enrichment provider can change the metadata visible for an already retrieved record without changing the primary retrieval set.
This distinction should remain explicit in both software and methods documentation.

12.6 Canonical Model Evolution
The canonical Paper model may evolve as additional metadata requirements emerge.
New fields should be introduced when they provide a clear shared role in retrieval, aggregation, detail presentation, enrichment, or export processing.
Changes to the canonical model should preserve backward compatibility where practical because Paper records can also occur in temporary cached or serialized application state.
The addition of a canonical field does not automatically imply that:
• every retrieval source supplies the field
• every enrichment provider supplies the field
• every user interface displays the field
• every export format serializes the field

These behaviors should be documented and validated separately.

12.7 All Sources Evolution
The All Sources workflow is expected to evolve as connectors and retrieval capabilities change.
Changes may include:
• addition or removal of participating sources
• changes in candidate depth
• filter propagation
• deduplication rules
• metadata merging
• ordering logic
• snapshot behavior
• partial-failure handling

Such changes can materially affect the composition or ordering of a federated result set and should therefore be treated as methodological changes rather than purely internal refactoring.
By contrast, implementation refactoring that preserves the same externally observable retrieval semantics does not necessarily require a methodological revision.

12.8 Validation and Change Control
Changes that affect retrieval methodology should be accompanied by appropriate validation.
The preferred development process is:
1. identify the intended behavioral change
2. inspect the existing implementation and tests
3. add or update regression coverage
4. implement the smallest required change
5. execute targeted tests
6. execute broader automated tests
7. perform live or manual sanity checks where appropriate
8. review the change through version control
9. update methodological documentation when required

This process connects software change control with methodological traceability.

12.9 Methods Paper Versioning
The Living Methods Paper should receive a new version when substantive documentation changes are required to reflect the implemented system.
A new methods-paper version may be appropriate when changes include:
• addition or removal of primary retrieval sources
• substantial changes to federated retrieval
• new controlled-vocabulary functionality
• material changes to deduplication
• new metadata-enrichment architecture
• substantial changes to export behavior
• changes to the canonical data model
• methodological changes to filtering, sorting, or pagination

Minor editorial corrections that do not change the described methodology need not necessarily constitute a new methodological version.

12.10 Relationship between Software and Methods Versions
The methods-paper version and the software version serve related but distinct purposes.
A software version identifies a particular state of the application.
A methods-paper version identifies a particular documented description of the methodology.
The two version sequences therefore do not need to be numerically identical.
However, a published or archived methods-paper version should identify the software version, release, or source-code state to which its implementation-specific claims apply whenever practical.
This relationship improves traceability between methodological documentation and executable software.

12.11 Reproducibility across Versions
A literature search should ideally be documented with sufficient information to identify:
• the query
• retrieval sources
• filters
• controlled-vocabulary parameters
• sorting mode
• retrieval mode where applicable
• retrieval date
• relevant export parameters
• LitSearch software version or source-code state

When a later LitSearch version changes retrieval behavior, the same visible query parameters should not automatically be assumed to represent an identical computational procedure.
Version information is therefore part of rigorous procedural reproducibility.

12.12 Historical Transparency
A Living Methods Paper should describe the current implementation without obscuring earlier methodological states.
Version-controlled source code, release documentation, and methods-paper revisions can together provide a historical record of how LitSearch evolved.
For example, changes such as:
• adding new retrieval sources
• changing source capabilities
• extending MeSH support
• improving deduplication
• introducing metadata enrichment
• modifying All Sources behavior

should be traceable to corresponding software and documentation revisions where practical.
This allows later users or researchers to determine which methodology applied at a particular stage of the project.

12.13 Current Documentation State
Version 1.3 of this Living Methods Paper describes the LitSearch implementation state developed through October 2026.
Relative to earlier documentation, this version incorporates methodological changes including:
• six primary retrieval sources: PubMed, Europe PMC, OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Crossref, and DOAJ
• explicit source and retrieval-mode capabilities
• expanded All Sources aggregation
• improved cross-source deduplication and metadata merging
• MeSH refinement for PubMed and Europe PMC
• temporary snapshot-based All Sources pagination
• a generic metadata-enrichment architecture
• PubMed-derived MeSH metadata enrichment
• OpenCitations citation and reference count enrichment
• clearer documentation of export schemas and their limitations

The v0.6.0 software development line remains subject to its own release-level validation and release process.
Accordingly, documentation of implemented functionality should not itself be interpreted as a declaration that a particular software release has completed all release-validation requirements.

12.14 Maintenance Principle
The Living Methods Paper should be updated when implementation changes alter the methodological interpretation of LitSearch.
The objective is not to document every internal code change.
Instead, documentation should remain synchronized with behavior that affects:
• what is searched
• how it is searched
• which records can be retrieved
• how records are normalized
• how records are combined
• how duplicates are resolved
• how metadata are enriched
• how results are ordered
• how results are exported
• how retrieval can be reproduced

This principle allows the documentation to evolve with the software while preserving a stable and auditable methodological core.

_______________________________________
13. Conclusion
LitSearch provides a modular framework for transparent, reproducible, and multi-source scientific literature retrieval.
The current implementation integrates six primary retrieval sources:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC
• OpenAlex
• Semantic Scholar
• Crossref
• DOAJ

Source-specific connectors preserve differences in query syntax, filtering, sorting, pagination, metadata structure, and operational constraints while translating retrieved records into a shared canonical Paper representation.
The All Sources workflow extends this architecture through bounded federated candidate retrieval, centralized cross-source deduplication, metadata merging, deterministic ordering, and temporary snapshot-based pagination.
Persistent identifiers such as DOI, PMID, and PMCID are used where available to support duplicate detection, with constrained bibliographic metadata matching providing additional deterministic fallback behavior.
Controlled-vocabulary retrieval is explicitly source-aware. MeSH refinement is supported for PubMed and Europe PMC, while sources without equivalent MeSH functionality retain their own retrieval semantics.
LitSearch additionally separates primary literature retrieval from metadata enrichment. Retrieved canonical records can be augmented through independent enrichment providers without introducing additional publications into the result set. The current implementation includes PubMed-derived MeSH metadata enrichment and OpenCitations citation and reference count enrichment for applicable detail records.
Structured CSV, RIS, and XLSX exports support downstream bibliographic workflows while preserving a defined subset of normalized retrieval metadata.
The system is designed to make computational retrieval procedures inspectable and reconstructable, while acknowledging that externally maintained scholarly databases remain dynamic and heterogeneous.
LitSearch does not perform study screening, critical appraisal, evidence grading, or evidence synthesis. These activities remain the responsibility of researchers and review teams.
The methodological contribution of LitSearch is therefore not automated scientific interpretation, but a transparent computational layer between researcher-defined search strategies and heterogeneous scholarly retrieval infrastructures.
By combining explicit query construction, source-aware capabilities, canonical normalization, federated retrieval, deterministic bibliographic processing, provenance-aware enrichment, structured export, and layered validation, LitSearch provides an extensible foundation for reproducible literature identification while preserving researcher control over subsequent scientific judgment.

_____________________________________
Appendix A — Technical Architecture and Extension Framework
This appendix describes the implementation architecture underlying the LitSearch methodology.
The technical architecture is organized around explicit separation of responsibilities:
• source-specific retrieval
• source and mode capability declaration
• canonical metadata normalization
• federated candidate aggregation
• cross-source deduplication and metadata merging
• deterministic result ordering
• temporary retrieval state
• metadata enrichment
• export generation
• presentation

The purpose of this separation is to allow individual components to evolve without requiring source-specific behavior to be duplicated throughout the application.

A.1 Application Architecture
LitSearch is implemented as a Python web application.
The principal technologies include:
• Python
• FastAPI
• Jinja2
• asynchronous HTTP and application workflows
• Redis
• ARQ
• openpyxl
• pytest
• Docker and Docker Compose

FastAPI provides the web application and routing layer.
Jinja2 is used for server-rendered application templates.
Redis supports temporary operational state, including pagination-related state, All Sources snapshots, and background-job coordination.
ARQ provides asynchronous background processing for workflows such as export generation.
External scholarly APIs are accessed through source-specific connector modules.

A.2 Retrieval Connector Layer
Each primary literature source is represented by source-specific connector logic.
The current primary retrieval sources are:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC
• OpenAlex
• Semantic Scholar
• Crossref
• DOAJ

A connector is responsible for translating LitSearch retrieval parameters into operations understood by the corresponding external API.
Depending on the source, connector responsibilities can include:
• free-text query construction
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• controlled-vocabulary filtering
• sort translation
• pagination
• API request execution
• rate-limit handling
• retry behavior
• response parsing
• canonical metadata normalization
• detail retrieval

Source-specific behavior should remain isolated within the connector or associated source-specific logic wherever practical.

A.3 Connector Capability Registry

LitSearch maintains a centralized capability registry describing supported search behavior for each retrieval source and, where required, individual retrieval modes.
The core search-mode capability representation contains:
• pagination_type
• supported_sorts
• supports_previous
• supports_last
• supports_page_jump
• supports_year_filter
• supports_abstract_filter
• supports_mesh_filter
• max_result_window

The currently represented pagination types are:
• page
• cursor
• token
• snapshot

Capabilities are descriptive of LitSearch behavior for the corresponding source and mode. They should not automatically be interpreted as a statement that an upstream API implements the behavior through a native mechanism.
For example, a LitSearch-level filtering capability may involve connector-side post-retrieval processing rather than a native upstream filter.

A.4 Current Capability Structure
The current source and mode structure can be summarized as follows.
All Sources:
• pagination: snapshot
• sorts: relevance, date_desc, date_asc
• previous: supported
• last: supported
• page jump: supported
• year filter: supported
• abstract filter: supported
• MeSH filter: not exposed as a full-result-set capability

PubMed:
• pagination: page
• sorts: relevance, date_desc
• previous: supported
• last: supported
• page jump: supported
• year filter: supported
• abstract filter: supported
• MeSH filter: supported

Europe PMC:
• pagination: cursor
• sorts: relevance
• previous: supported
• last: supported
• page jump: supported within the implemented navigation workflow
• year filter: supported
• abstract filter: supported
• MeSH filter: supported

OpenAlex:
• pagination: page
• sorts: relevance, date_desc, date_asc
• previous: supported
• last: supported
• page jump: supported
• year filter: supported
• abstract filter: supported
• MeSH filter: not supported

Crossref:
• pagination: page
• sorts: relevance, date_desc, date_asc
• previous: supported
• last: supported
• page jump: supported
• year filter: supported
• abstract filter: supported
• MeSH filter: not supported

DOAJ:
• pagination: page
• sorts: relevance
• previous: supported
• last: supported
• page jump: supported
• year filter: supported
• abstract filter: supported
• MeSH filter: not supported

Semantic Scholar relevance mode:
• pagination: page
• sorts: relevance
• previous: supported
• last: not supported
• page jump: not supported
• year filter: not exposed as a full-result-set capability
• abstract filter: supported at LitSearch level
• MeSH filter: not supported

Semantic Scholar bulk mode:
• pagination: token
• sorts: date_desc, date_asc
• previous: supported through application state
• last: not supported
• page jump: not supported
• year filter: supported
• abstract filter: supported at LitSearch level
• MeSH filter: not supported

The registry can additionally contain max_result_window metadata for selected modes. Such metadata should be distinguished from export limits or request page sizes.

A.5 Canonical Paper Representation
Source-specific records are normalized into a shared Paper representation.
The current canonical fields are:
• id
• source
• title
• authors
• journal
• year
• publication_date
• abstract
• doi
• pmcid
• url
• mesh_terms
• has_full_text
• concepts
• citation_count
• reference_count
• enrichment_sources

Not every source supplies every field.
The canonical model therefore represents a common structural interface rather than a completeness requirement.
For PubMed records, the PubMed identifier can be represented through id. The current canonical model does not define a separate pmid attribute.

A.6 Normalization Boundary
Normalization occurs between source-specific response handling and shared downstream processing.
Conceptually:
External API response
↓
Source-specific parsing
↓
Canonical Paper
↓
Shared processing

This boundary prevents downstream aggregation, deduplication, presentation, and export code from depending directly on every external API schema.
Source-specific metadata can still be omitted when no appropriate canonical representation exists.
Normalization should therefore not be interpreted as lossless preservation of the complete upstream API response.

A.7 All Sources Retrieval Pipeline
All Sources is implemented as a centralized federated candidate-retrieval pipeline.
The current participating sources are:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

Candidate retrieval is performed concurrently where possible.
Conceptually:
Shared LitSearch search state
↓
Source-specific candidate retrieval
↓
Canonical Paper records
↓
Combined candidate collection
↓
Cross-source deduplication
↓
Metadata merging
↓
Central result ordering
↓
Temporary snapshot
↓
Page slicing

All Sources retrieves bounded candidate sets rather than exhaustively harvesting all matching records from every source.
The candidate depth is determined by the requested workflow and configured candidate-retrieval settings.

A.8 All Sources Filter Propagation
All Sources translates common search state into source-specific connector calls.
Current filter propagation is not completely uniform.
Publication-year and abstract parameters are propagated to:
• PubMed
• OpenAlex
• Crossref
• DOAJ
• Europe PMC

MeSH parameters and the selected MeSH logical mode are propagated to:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC

Semantic Scholar currently participates in All Sources relevance retrieval without the same year and abstract parameter propagation used for those other connectors.
Consequently, the federated pipeline should not be interpreted as applying every filter identically to every source.
The All Sources capability model therefore distinguishes user-facing full-result-set capabilities from partial source-specific behavior.

A.9 Partial Source Failure
Each source retrieval operation is isolated within the federated candidate workflow.
If an individual connector raises an exception during All Sources candidate retrieval, the source can contribute an empty candidate set while the remaining sources continue.
The processed state can retain:
• per-source record counts
• failed-source information

This allows LitSearch to return a partial federated result rather than failing the complete search because one external service is temporarily unavailable.
A partial result should nevertheless be interpreted as incomplete source participation.

A.10 Cross-Source Deduplication
All Sources uses centralized deterministic deduplication after candidate aggregation.
Identifier-based matching prioritizes normalized persistent identifiers:
1. DOI
2. PMID
3. PMCID

DOI normalization reduces superficial formatting differences such as resolver prefixes, DOI labels, letter case, and selected trailing punctuation.
When identifier matching is unavailable or insufficient, bibliographic metadata can contribute to duplicate detection.
Current strict metadata comparison can use:
• normalized title
• normalized first author
• publication year

A constrained tolerant comparison can accommodate a one-year publication-year discrepancy under additional identifier safeguards.

A.11 Identifier Conflict Safeguards
Metadata similarity does not override conflicting persistent identifiers.
When two records both contain a DOI, PMID, or PMCID and the corresponding identifiers conflict, the records are protected from metadata-based merging according to the deduplication rules.
This reduces the risk that records with similar titles, authors, and years but distinct persistent identities are incorrectly consolidated.
The deduplication architecture therefore combines positive matching rules with explicit conflict protection.

A.12 Cross-Source Metadata Merging
When two retrieval records are identified as duplicates, LitSearch can merge complementary metadata.
The merge strategy preserves existing populated metadata in the primary record and fills missing values from the matched record where applicable.
Source provenance is combined so that the resulting record can represent contribution from multiple retrieval sources.
Conceptually:
Primary Paper
+
Matched duplicate Paper
↓
Preserve populated primary values
↓
Fill missing metadata
↓
Combine source provenance
↓
Merged Paper

This process is distinct from enrichment-provider merging.

Cross-source merging combines records already present in the retrieval candidate set.

A.13 Central Result Ordering
After deduplication and metadata merging, All Sources applies centralized ordering.
For chronological modes, normalized publication-year metadata are used for ascending or descending ordering, with deterministic secondary values where required.
For relevance-oriented results, LitSearch does not combine heterogeneous upstream relevance scores.
Instead, records retain their source-specific candidate order and are interleaved in deterministic round-robin order.
The current source order is:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

This produces deterministic source-balanced presentation without asserting a common relevance metric across databases.

A.14 All Sources Snapshot State
Processed All Sources results can be stored temporarily in Redis as a snapshot.
Snapshot state includes information such as:
• ordered Paper records
• duplicates_removed
• source_counts
• failed_sources
• request_signature

The request signature is derived from relevant retrieval state, including:
• query
• sort
• candidate size
• year filters
• abstract filter
• MeSH state
• MeSH logical mode

A compatible snapshot allows subsequent result pages to be sliced from the same processed ordered collection rather than rebuilding the federated search for every page request.
The default snapshot lifetime is approximately 900 seconds and can be configured.
Snapshots are operational retrieval state rather than permanent archival records.

A.15 Metadata Enrichment Architecture
Metadata enrichment is implemented separately from primary retrieval and cross-source aggregation.
Conceptually:
Retrieved canonical Paper
↓
Configured enrichment provider
↓
EnrichmentResult
↓
Controlled merge
↓
Enriched canonical Paper

An enrichment provider can inspect record identifiers and retrieve supplementary metadata without introducing additional papers into the result set.
Provider failures are isolated so that unsuccessful enrichment does not invalidate the underlying retrieved record.

A.16 Current Enrichment Providers
The current implementation includes two enrichment functions of methodological relevance.
PubMed-derived MeSH enrichment:
• obtains MeSH descriptor metadata for applicable records
• can use a PubMed identifier directly for PubMed records
• can resolve suitable DOI-identified non-PubMed records to PubMed identifiers
• adds MeSH metadata to an already retrieved record

OpenCitations enrichment:
• requires a DOI
• can retrieve citation_count
• can retrieve reference_count
• treats the two counts independently according to available provider metadata

Neither provider acts as a primary literature retrieval source.

A.17 Enrichment Merge Rules
The generic enrichment merge layer controls which metadata can modify the canonical Paper.
Current supported list fields include:
• mesh_terms
• concepts

Current supported scalar fields include:
• abstract
• citation_count
• reference_count

For scalar fields, an existing populated canonical value is preserved.
For list fields, new values can be combined with existing values using case-insensitive duplicate detection while preserving deterministic value order and existing representation where possible.
Fields outside the supported enrichment merge set are ignored by the generic merge operation.
Provider provenance is recorded only for fields actually changed by enrichment.

A.18 Enrichment Provenance
The canonical enrichment_sources field maps enriched metadata fields to the provider or providers responsible for modifying those fields.
This mechanism provides field-level provenance for enrichment operations.
It is not a complete provenance graph for every metadata field in Paper.
Primary retrieval provenance is represented separately through source attribution and, for merged federated records, combined source information.

Thus:
source
→ retrieval provenance
enrichment_sources
→ provenance of fields modified through enrichment

These two provenance mechanisms serve different purposes.

A.19 Detail Workflow
Metadata enrichment currently participates primarily in the individual paper-detail workflow.
Conceptually:
Source-specific detail retrieval
↓
Canonical Paper
↓
Configured enrichment pipeline
↓
Enriched Paper
↓
Detail presentation

The enrichment layer should therefore not be interpreted as a mandatory processing stage for every search-result list or standard export.
This separation also prevents enrichment-service availability from determining whether primary search results can be displayed.

A.20 Export Architecture
LitSearch currently supports:
• CSV
• RIS
• XLSX

Export generation operates on normalized Paper records but serializes selected bibliographic fields rather than the complete canonical model.
CSV and XLSX currently contain:
• ID
• Source
• Title
• Authors
• Journal
• Year
• DOI
• PMCID
• URL

RIS maps a corresponding bibliographic subset into RIS tags.
Metadata such as citation counts, reference counts, MeSH terms, concepts, and enrichment provenance are not currently part of the standard export schemas.

A.21 Background Export Processing
Longer-running export operations can be delegated to ARQ background jobs.
Redis supports the operational state required for job coordination.
Conceptually:
HTTP export request
↓
Job creation
↓
ARQ worker
↓
Retrieval and processing
↓
File generation
↓
Completed export

This architecture avoids requiring a long-running export to remain entirely within a single synchronous web request.

A.22 Caching and Temporary State
Redis is used for several forms of operational state.
Depending on the workflow, this can include:
• retrieval caches
• pagination state
• Europe PMC cursor state
• All Sources snapshots
• export-job state
• related temporary workflow metadata

Cache and state identifiers are derived from relevant normalized request parameters where applicable so that incompatible retrieval states are not intentionally reused as equivalent searches.
Redis should not be interpreted as permanent storage of the scholarly literature or as a long-term reproducibility archive.
A.23 Extension Requirements for New Retrieval Sources
A new retrieval source should implement the source-specific behavior required by its API while integrating with shared LitSearch abstractions.
A typical source integration should address:
1. API communication
2. query construction
3. pagination
4. sort translation
5. supported filters
6. response parsing
7. canonical Paper normalization
8. detail retrieval where applicable
9. operational error handling
10. capability declaration
11. automated validation

If the source is intended to participate in All Sources, additional integration should address:
12. federated candidate retrieval
13. filter propagation
14. source ordering
15. deduplication compatibility
16. snapshot/request-signature compatibility
17. export workflow compatibility
18. partial-failure behavior

A source should not be added to All Sources solely because it can produce Paper objects. Federated participation also requires explicit integration with the aggregation workflow.

A.24 Extension Requirements for New Enrichment Providers
An enrichment provider has a different contract from a retrieval connector.
A typical enrichment provider should define:
1. record matching requirements
2. required identifiers
3. provider request behavior
4. metadata fields supplied
5. matched or unmatched result behavior
6. error handling
7. compatibility with enrichment merge rules
8. provider provenance
9. automated validation

An enrichment provider should augment an existing Paper without creating additional search-result records.
This architectural distinction allows bibliographic enrichment to evolve independently from retrieval-source coverage.

A.25 Architectural Invariants
Despite source-specific differences, the following architectural invariants should remain stable:
• external retrieval behavior remains source-aware
• retrieved records are normalized before shared downstream processing
• source capabilities are represented explicitly
• federated aggregation is distinct from individual source retrieval
• duplicate detection is deterministic and inspectable
• metadata merging does not imply scientific appraisal
• enrichment is separate from primary retrieval
• enrichment provider failure does not invalidate primary retrieval
• export schemas are explicit
• temporary application state is not treated as permanent archival storage

These invariants form the technical foundation of the LitSearch methodological model.

A.26 High-Level Architecture
The complete architecture can be represented conceptually as:
Researcher-defined search state
↓
Capability-aware source selection
↓
Source-specific query construction
↓
External scholarly APIs
↓
Source-specific response parsing
↓
Canonical Paper normalization
↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Source-specific workflow            │
│ or                                  │
│ All Sources candidate aggregation   │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
↓
Cross-source deduplication and metadata merging
(where applicable)
↓
Deterministic ordering
↓
Temporary retrieval state / pagination
↓
Search-result presentation
↓
Individual detail retrieval
↓
Optional metadata enrichment
↓
Detail presentation

In parallel, normalized retrieval records can enter:
Normalized Paper records
↓
Export processing
↓
CSV / RIS / XLSX

This architecture preserves a clear separation between retrieval, normalization, aggregation, enrichment, presentation, and export while allowing each layer to evolve independently under explicit methodological constraints.________________________________________


______________________________________
Appendix B — OpenAlex Source Profile
This appendix documents the implementation-specific behavior of the OpenAlex connector in LitSearch.
OpenAlex is integrated as a primary multidisciplinary retrieval source. Its connector translates LitSearch search parameters into OpenAlex API requests and normalizes returned works into the canonical Paper representation.

B.1 Role within LitSearch
OpenAlex provides multidisciplinary scholarly metadata and complements the biomedical focus of PubMed and Europe PMC.
Within LitSearch, OpenAlex functions as:

• an independent primary retrieval source
• a participant in All Sources federated retrieval
• a source of bibliographic and concept metadata
• a source of records for centralized cross-source deduplication
• a source compatible with the shared export architecture

OpenAlex is a retrieval source rather than an enrichment provider.

B.2 Retrieval Interface
The OpenAlex connector performs literature retrieval through the OpenAlex API.
The current integration supports:
• free-text search
• publication-year filtering
• abstract-availability filtering
• relevance ordering
• newest-first chronological ordering
• oldest-first chronological ordering
• page-oriented navigation in the LitSearch interface

The connector translates these LitSearch parameters into the corresponding OpenAlex request parameters.
Returned records are normalized into canonical Paper objects before shared downstream processing.

B.3 Free-Text Retrieval
A LitSearch free-text query is transmitted through the OpenAlex search functionality.
OpenAlex determines the source-specific relevance ordering of records returned for relevance-oriented retrieval.
LitSearch does not attempt to reinterpret the internal OpenAlex relevance algorithm.
For a source-specific OpenAlex search, the ordering returned by the corresponding OpenAlex retrieval operation forms the basis for presentation.
When OpenAlex participates in All Sources relevance retrieval, its candidate ordering is preserved within the OpenAlex source bucket before centralized interleaving.

B.4 Publication-Year Filtering
OpenAlex supports publication-year filtering within the current LitSearch integration.
Year restrictions are translated into OpenAlex filter parameters before the request is executed.
The year filter can therefore constrain the OpenAlex retrieval operation upstream rather than requiring LitSearch to retrieve an unfiltered result page and discard records solely on the basis of year afterward.
As with other sources, the meaning of publication year remains dependent on the metadata supplied by the upstream database.

B.5 Abstract-Availability Filtering
OpenAlex supports native abstract-availability filtering in the current LitSearch connector.
When the user requests records with abstracts, the connector applies:
has_abstract:true
through the OpenAlex filter mechanism.
This is an upstream retrieval constraint.
It should therefore be distinguished from connectors or retrieval modes in which abstract filtering is performed locally after records have already been returned by an external API.
The presence of the OpenAlex abstract-availability filter does not guarantee that every returned abstract has equivalent length, structure, or metadata quality.

B.6 Sorting
The current OpenAlex integration supports three user-facing sort modes:
• relevance
• date_desc
• date_asc

These correspond conceptually to:
• relevance-oriented retrieval
• most recent first
• oldest first

For chronological retrieval, LitSearch translates the selected mode into the corresponding OpenAlex publication-date ordering.
The capability registry exposes these three sort modes for OpenAlex.
Unsupported sort behavior should not be synthesized outside the declared capability model.

B.7 Pagination
OpenAlex is represented as a page-based retrieval source in the LitSearch capability model.
The current OpenAlex mode supports:
• previous-page navigation
• last-page navigation
• direct page jumping

Pagination state is translated into the appropriate OpenAlex request behavior by the connector and surrounding retrieval workflow.
Page-oriented interaction should not be interpreted as permanent preservation of an upstream result set. If OpenAlex content or ranking changes between requests, repeated retrieval may produce different records.

B.8 Canonical Metadata Mapping
OpenAlex records are transformed into the canonical Paper representation.
Depending on metadata availability, normalized fields can include:
• source identifier
• source
• title
• authors
• journal or venue
• publication year
• publication date
• abstract
• DOI
• URL
• subject concepts
• full-text availability information where derivable

Fields that are unavailable remain empty or unset as appropriate.
OpenAlex does not supply LitSearch MeSH retrieval functionality.
MeSH terms should therefore not be synthesized from OpenAlex concepts.

B.9 Concept Metadata
OpenAlex can provide subject or concept metadata associated with scholarly works.
Where suitable metadata are available, LitSearch can normalize these values into the canonical concepts field.
These concepts are source-specific subject metadata.
They should not be interpreted as equivalent to Medical Subject Headings.
In particular:
OpenAlex concepts
≠
MeSH descriptors

This distinction is important because LitSearch uses MeSH as an explicit controlled-vocabulary retrieval mechanism only for compatible biomedical sources.

B.10 MeSH Capability
The OpenAlex connector does not support LitSearch MeSH filtering.
Its capability declaration therefore sets MeSH filtering as unsupported.
When an All Sources search involves MeSH parameters, those parameters can be propagated to PubMed and Europe PMC but are not translated into an artificial OpenAlex equivalent.
This preserves source-specific semantics and prevents OpenAlex concept metadata from being misrepresented as MeSH retrieval.

B.11 Participation in All Sources
OpenAlex is one of the six current sources participating in the All Sources candidate-retrieval pipeline.
The current source set is:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

For All Sources, OpenAlex receives a bounded candidate request based on the federated retrieval workflow.
Applicable publication-year and abstract-availability parameters are propagated to the OpenAlex connector.
Returned records are normalized before entering the combined candidate collection.

B.12 Cross-Source Deduplication
OpenAlex records participate in the centralized All Sources deduplication process.
Where available, DOI provides a strong persistent-identifier basis for matching an OpenAlex record with corresponding records from other retrieval sources.
Deduplication is not limited to DOI matching.
The centralized LitSearch process can additionally use:
• PMID where represented and applicable
• PMCID where represented and applicable
• normalized title
• normalized first author
• publication year

Metadata-based matching is constrained by persistent-identifier conflict safeguards.
A limited one-year publication-year tolerance can additionally be used under the conditions defined by the central deduplication algorithm.
OpenAlex therefore does not implement a separate source-specific duplicate-resolution methodology.

B.13 Metadata Merging
When an OpenAlex record is identified as a duplicate of a record from another retrieval source, centralized metadata merging can fill missing values while preserving already populated primary metadata.
Source attribution can be combined to retain provenance from contributing retrieval sources.
This operation occurs within the shared cross-source processing layer.
It is distinct from metadata enrichment through providers such as OpenCitations.

B.14 Relevance within All Sources
OpenAlex relevance scores are not normalized against scores from PubMed, Crossref, DOAJ, Europe PMC, or Semantic Scholar.
For All Sources relevance-oriented presentation, the OpenAlex candidate order is retained within its source bucket.
The source buckets are then interleaved using the deterministic All Sources source order.
Consequently, the final federated position of an OpenAlex record should not be interpreted as a globally normalized OpenAlex-versus-other-source relevance score.

B.15 Chronological All Sources Ordering
When All Sources uses date_desc or date_asc, ordering is performed centrally after candidate aggregation, deduplication, and metadata merging.
The federated workflow therefore does not require chronological values returned by heterogeneous APIs to be treated as directly comparable source-specific relevance signals.
Normalized publication-year metadata provide the principal basis for central chronological ordering.
OpenAlex records participate in this process through their normalized canonical metadata.

B.16 Detail Retrieval
The OpenAlex integration supports source-specific paper detail retrieval.
A retrieved OpenAlex detail record is normalized into a canonical Paper before presentation.
The resulting Paper can subsequently enter the generic detail-page enrichment pipeline.
This means that an OpenAlex record with a suitable DOI may, where applicable, receive metadata from enrichment providers independently of the fact that the original record was retrieved from OpenAlex.
The retrieval source and enrichment provider therefore remain methodologically distinct.

B.17 Export
OpenAlex records can participate in the standard LitSearch export workflows.
Current export formats include:
• CSV
• RIS
• XLSX

Exports use normalized Paper records but serialize the selected export schema rather than every OpenAlex or canonical metadata field.
For example, OpenAlex concept metadata are not currently included as a standard field in all supported exports.
The same distinction applies to metadata later added through enrichment.

B.18 Error and Availability Boundaries
OpenAlex is an external service and remains outside LitSearch operational control.
Retrieval can therefore be affected by:
• network failures
• timeouts
• rate limiting
• upstream service errors
• API changes
• metadata changes

In a source-specific OpenAlex search, such failures can prevent successful retrieval.
Within All Sources, an OpenAlex retrieval failure can be recorded as a failed source while other participating sources continue contributing candidates.
A partial federated result should not be interpreted as equivalent to successful participation by all configured sources.

B.19 Methodological Interpretation
The OpenAlex connector illustrates the LitSearch source-integration model:
LitSearch search state
↓
OpenAlex-specific parameter translation
↓
OpenAlex API retrieval
↓
OpenAlex response parsing
↓
Canonical Paper normalization
↓
Source-specific presentation
or
All Sources aggregation

This design allows OpenAlex-specific retrieval behavior to remain isolated while normalized records participate in shared LitSearch processing.
The integration does not attempt to make OpenAlex methodologically identical to PubMed, Europe PMC, Semantic Scholar, Crossref, or DOAJ.
Instead, LitSearch preserves source-specific retrieval semantics while providing a common structural framework for inspection, aggregation, deduplication, and export.

B.20 Summary
OpenAlex functions as a multidisciplinary primary retrieval source within LitSearch.
The current integration provides:
• free-text retrieval
• upstream publication-year filtering
• native abstract-availability filtering
• relevance and bidirectional chronological sorting
• page-oriented navigation
• canonical Paper normalization
• concept metadata where available
• source-specific detail retrieval
• participation in All Sources
• centralized cross-source deduplication and metadata merging
• compatibility with standard LitSearch export workflows

OpenAlex does not provide LitSearch MeSH retrieval.
Its relevance ordering remains source-specific and is not numerically normalized against relevance signals from other literature databases.
This profile preserves OpenAlex-specific retrieval behavior while allowing its records to participate in the shared reproducible LitSearch architecture.

______________________________
Appendix C — Semantic Scholar Source Profile
This appendix documents the implementation-specific behavior of the Semantic Scholar connector in LitSearch.
Semantic Scholar is integrated as a primary multidisciplinary retrieval source with two distinct retrieval modes:
• relevance mode
• bulk chronological mode

These modes use different Semantic Scholar API mechanisms and therefore have different pagination, sorting, and filtering semantics.

C.1 Role within LitSearch
Semantic Scholar functions as:
• an independent primary retrieval source
• a participant in All Sources federated retrieval
• a source of normalized scholarly metadata
• a source of records for centralized cross-source deduplication
• a source compatible with the shared export architecture

Semantic Scholar is a retrieval source rather than an enrichment provider.
Its two retrieval modes are represented separately in the LitSearch capability model because they do not provide equivalent behavior.

C.2 Relevance Mode
Relevance mode uses the Semantic Scholar paper-search endpoint.
This mode is intended for relevance-oriented free-text retrieval.
The current LitSearch capability model represents relevance mode as:
• pagination type: page
• supported sort: relevance
• previous navigation: supported
• last navigation: not supported
• direct page jumping: not supported
• year filter: not exposed as a full-result-set capability
• abstract filter: supported at LitSearch level
• MeSH filter: not supported
• maximum result-window metadata: 1000

The page-oriented LitSearch interface is backed by Semantic Scholar's offset-based search behavior.

C.3 Relevance Retrieval
A free-text query is submitted to the Semantic Scholar search API.
Semantic Scholar determines the upstream relevance ordering.
LitSearch does not attempt to reconstruct or normalize Semantic Scholar's internal relevance score.
For source-specific relevance retrieval, records therefore retain the ordering derived from the Semantic Scholar search operation, subject to any LitSearch-side processing required by the active workflow.

C.4 Relevance-Mode Pagination
Semantic Scholar relevance search uses offset-oriented upstream pagination.
LitSearch presents this through its page-based application model.
This mode does not expose:
• last-page navigation
• arbitrary direct page jumping

through the current capability declaration.
These restrictions reflect the implemented navigation semantics rather than an assumption that all page-oriented APIs must provide equivalent navigation controls.

C.5 Relevance-Mode Year Filtering
Publication-year handling in Semantic Scholar relevance mode differs from the upstream filtering strategies used by several other LitSearch connectors.
In the current source-specific implementation, year filtering can be applied locally to records returned by the relevance search.
This means that the filter operates on the retrieved candidate page rather than redefining the complete upstream Semantic Scholar result set.
Consequently, relevance mode does not declare publication-year filtering as a full-result-set capability.
This distinction is methodologically important.
A locally filtered page is not equivalent to an upstream query constrained to the requested year interval before ranking and pagination.

C.6 Relevance-Mode Abstract Filtering
Abstract-availability filtering in Semantic Scholar relevance mode is performed locally after records have been retrieved.
Records without a usable abstract can therefore be removed from the returned candidate set.
The capability is exposed at LitSearch level, but its implementation should not be interpreted as a native Semantic Scholar abstract filter.
Local filtering can also cause the number of records displayed after filtering to differ from the number initially retrieved from the upstream result page.

C.7 Bulk Chronological Mode
Semantic Scholar bulk search is used for chronological retrieval.
The current capability model represents bulk mode as:
• pagination type: token
• supported sorts: date_desc, date_asc
• previous navigation: supported through LitSearch state
• last navigation: not supported
• direct page jumping: not supported
• year filter: supported
• abstract filter: supported at LitSearch level
• MeSH filter: not supported

Bulk mode is methodologically distinct from relevance mode and should not be treated merely as a different visual sort of the same retrieval procedure.

C.8 Bulk-Mode Sorting
Bulk mode supports chronological retrieval in both directions:
• date_desc — most recent first
• date_asc — oldest first

The selected LitSearch sort is translated into the corresponding Semantic Scholar bulk-search behavior.
Relevance sorting is not represented as a bulk-mode capability.
Similarly, chronological sorting is not represented as a relevance-mode capability.
The user-facing sort selection therefore also determines which Semantic Scholar retrieval mode is appropriate.

C.9 Bulk-Mode Pagination
Semantic Scholar bulk search uses continuation tokens rather than arbitrary page offsets.
LitSearch therefore models this mode as token pagination.
A returned token identifies continuation state for subsequent retrieval.
This has several consequences:
• arbitrary direct page jumping is not supported
• a mathematically derived last page is not exposed
• forward navigation depends on continuation state
• previous navigation requires application-maintained state rather than a native numerical page calculation

Token pagination should therefore not be described as equivalent to conventional page-number pagination.

C.10 Bulk-Mode Year Filtering
Publication-year filtering is supported upstream in the Semantic Scholar bulk workflow.
This allows the bulk request itself to constrain records by year before LitSearch performs subsequent processing.
The capability registry therefore exposes year filtering for bulk mode.
This differs from relevance mode, where year filtering is currently performed locally on retrieved records and is not exposed as a full-result-set capability.

C.11 Bulk-Mode Abstract Filtering
Abstract filtering in the current Semantic Scholar bulk workflow is performed locally.
Records returned by the upstream operation can be inspected and records without suitable abstract metadata removed.
The LitSearch-level abstract capability therefore does not imply that Semantic Scholar performs this filtering natively.
This distinction is preserved in the methods documentation because upstream and post-retrieval filters can have different effects on result counts and pagination.

C.12 MeSH
Semantic Scholar does not provide LitSearch MeSH filtering.
The capability is therefore disabled in both relevance and bulk modes.
LitSearch does not translate MeSH descriptors into Semantic Scholar topics, fields of study, or other source-specific concepts as if they were equivalent controlled-vocabulary terms.
This prevents semantic substitution between distinct metadata systems.

C.13 Canonical Metadata Normalization
Semantic Scholar responses are transformed into canonical Paper records.
Depending on upstream availability, normalized metadata can include:
• source identifier
• source
• title
• authors
• journal or venue
• publication year
• publication date where available
• abstract
• DOI
• URL
• additional compatible canonical metadata

Unavailable values remain empty or unset as appropriate.
Normalization provides a common downstream representation without implying that Semantic Scholar and other retrieval sources expose identical metadata.

C.14 Participation in All Sources
Semantic Scholar participates in the six-source All Sources candidate-retrieval pipeline.
The current source set is:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

For current All Sources retrieval, Semantic Scholar is queried through relevance-oriented retrieval.
The federated workflow supplies the search query and candidate request size.
Current All Sources processing does not propagate the same publication-year and abstract parameters to Semantic Scholar that it propagates to PubMed, OpenAlex, Crossref, DOAJ, and Europe PMC.
This behavior is intentional in the present implementation.

C.15 All Sources Filter Consequences
Because Semantic Scholar currently receives different filter propagation within All Sources, federated year and abstract semantics are heterogeneous.
For the other participating connectors, applicable year and abstract restrictions can be incorporated into their candidate retrieval.
Semantic Scholar currently contributes relevance candidates without those same propagated parameters.

Consequently:
All Sources filter selection
≠
identical upstream filtering across all six sources

This limitation should be considered when interpreting filtered federated result sets.

C.16 All Sources Relevance Ordering
Semantic Scholar relevance scores are not normalized against scores from the other participating sources.
Within All Sources relevance retrieval, Semantic Scholar candidates retain their source-specific candidate order.
After candidate aggregation and deduplication, source buckets are interleaved deterministically.
Semantic Scholar currently occupies the sixth position in the fixed interleaving source order:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

This order is a deterministic processing rule.
It is not a ranking of source quality and does not imply that a Semantic Scholar result is intrinsically less relevant because its source bucket is processed sixth.

C.17 Cross-Source Deduplication
Semantic Scholar records participate in the centralized All Sources deduplication process.
Matching can use normalized persistent identifiers where available, prioritizing:
1. DOI
2. PMID
3. PMCID

Where direct identifier matching is unavailable, centralized metadata comparison can additionally use:
• normalized title
• normalized first author
• publication year

A constrained one-year publication-year tolerance can be used under additional identifier safeguards.
Conflicting persistent identifiers prevent metadata-based merging according to the central deduplication rules.
Semantic Scholar therefore does not require an independent source-specific deduplication methodology.

C.18 Metadata Merging
When a Semantic Scholar record is matched to a duplicate record from another retrieval source, LitSearch can preserve existing populated primary metadata and fill missing values from the matched record.
Retrieval-source provenance can be combined.
This process is distinct from enrichment-provider processing.
For example, a Semantic Scholar-derived Paper that subsequently receives citation metadata from OpenCitations remains a Semantic Scholar retrieval record with separately tracked enrichment provenance.

C.19 Rate Limiting
Semantic Scholar imposes operational constraints on API usage.
The current LitSearch connector incorporates request pacing to reduce the likelihood of exceeding the service's applicable request limits.
The implementation includes distributed coordination through Redis together with local timing safeguards.
The effective minimum interval is approximately 1.1 seconds between coordinated Semantic Scholar requests.
This is an operational safeguard rather than a guarantee that Semantic Scholar will accept every request.

C.20 Retry and Failure Handling

Semantic Scholar requests can fail because of:
• rate limiting
• authentication or authorization problems
• network timeouts
• upstream server errors
• service availability
• API-policy changes

The connector includes retry handling for selected transient conditions, including applicable timeout, rate-limit, and server-error responses.
Retries do not guarantee successful retrieval.
Persistent upstream rejection or service failure can still result in an unsuccessful Semantic Scholar request.

C.21 API-Key and Runtime Availability
Static connector capability and runtime API availability are separate concerns.
A capability declaration states what the LitSearch integration is designed to support.
It does not guarantee that the external Semantic Scholar service will accept a request at a particular moment.
For example, authentication state, provider-side throttling, or temporary service conditions can make the connector unavailable even though its declared retrieval capabilities remain unchanged.
This distinction applies generally to external sources but is especially important for services with restrictive runtime access conditions.

C.22 Partial Failure within All Sources
A Semantic Scholar failure does not necessarily invalidate the complete All Sources request.
If Semantic Scholar retrieval fails while the other source calls succeed, the federated workflow can continue with the remaining candidate sets and record Semantic Scholar among the failed sources.
The resulting search is therefore a partial federated result.
The failed-source state should be distinguished from a valid zero-result response.

C.23 Result Counts
Result-count interpretation requires particular care when LitSearch performs local post-retrieval filtering.
An upstream Semantic Scholar total can describe the source query before LitSearch applies local year or abstract filtering.
The number of records retained and displayed after local processing can therefore differ from the upstream total.
LitSearch should not treat these quantities as methodologically interchangeable.
This limitation is one reason why source-specific filter implementation is documented explicitly.

C.24 Detail Retrieval and Enrichment
Semantic Scholar records can participate in source-specific detail workflows where supported by the connector.
After normalization into a canonical Paper, an applicable detail record can enter the generic LitSearch enrichment pipeline.
For example, a Semantic Scholar-derived record containing a DOI may be eligible for OpenCitations enrichment.
The resulting citation or reference metadata originate from the enrichment provider, not from the Semantic Scholar retrieval operation unless separately supplied by the source.
Retrieval provenance and enrichment provenance therefore remain distinct.

C.25 Export
Semantic Scholar records can participate in the standard LitSearch export workflows.
Current formats are:
• CSV
• RIS
• XLSX

The export layer serializes the defined LitSearch export schema rather than the complete Semantic Scholar response or every field in the canonical Paper.
Consequently, metadata present during retrieval or later enrichment are not automatically included in exported files.

C.26 Methodological Interpretation
The Semantic Scholar integration can be represented conceptually as two related but distinct workflows.
Relevance workflow:
Free-text query
↓
Semantic Scholar relevance search
↓
Offset-based upstream retrieval
↓
Canonical normalization
↓
Applicable local filtering
↓
Page-oriented LitSearch presentation

Bulk chronological workflow:

Free-text query
↓
Semantic Scholar bulk search
↓
Upstream year restriction where applicable
↓
Token-based continuation
↓
Canonical normalization
↓
Applicable local abstract filtering
↓
Chronological LitSearch presentation

The two workflows should not be collapsed into a single generic pagination-and-sort model because their retrieval semantics differ.

C.27 Summary
Semantic Scholar functions as a multidisciplinary primary retrieval source within LitSearch.
Its integration deliberately distinguishes:
• relevance-oriented offset retrieval
• bulk token-based chronological retrieval

Relevance mode provides relevance sorting, page-oriented presentation, local year handling, and local abstract filtering.
Bulk mode provides chronological retrieval, upstream year filtering, token pagination, and local abstract filtering.
Neither mode supports MeSH retrieval.
Semantic Scholar participates in All Sources through relevance-oriented candidate retrieval, although the current federated workflow does not propagate the same year and abstract parameters to Semantic Scholar that are propagated to the other applicable connectors.
Its records participate in centralized normalization, deduplication, metadata merging, deterministic federated ordering, export, and applicable detail enrichment.
Operational rate limits and runtime API availability remain external constraints and are methodologically distinct from the static capabilities declared by LitSearch.

_____________________________________
Appendix D — Crossref Source Profile
This appendix documents the implementation-specific behavior of the Crossref connector in LitSearch.
Crossref is integrated as a primary multidisciplinary bibliographic retrieval source. The connector retrieves scholarly metadata through the Crossref REST API, translates LitSearch search parameters into Crossref request parameters, and normalizes returned works into the canonical Paper representation.

D.1 Role within LitSearch
Crossref functions as:
• an independent primary retrieval source
• a participant in All Sources federated retrieval
• a source of DOI-centered bibliographic metadata
• a source of records for centralized cross-source deduplication
• a source compatible with the shared detail and export architecture

Crossref is a retrieval source rather than an enrichment provider.
Its principal role in LitSearch is bibliographic discovery and metadata retrieval across disciplines.

D.2 API Interface
The current connector uses the Crossref REST API.
The principal API base is:

https://api.crossref.org

Literature search is performed through the works resource.
Conceptually:

LitSearch query
↓
Crossref /works
↓
Crossref metadata response
↓
Canonical Paper normalization

Individual DOI-based detail retrieval can use the corresponding Crossref works resource for that DOI.

D.3 Free-Text Retrieval
A LitSearch free-text query is transmitted through the Crossref works search interface.
The connector supplies the requested result count and offset required for the active page.
Crossref determines the upstream relevance behavior for the default relevance-oriented search.
LitSearch does not attempt to reconstruct Crossref's internal relevance algorithm.
For source-specific relevance retrieval, the returned Crossref candidate order therefore provides the basis for presentation.

D.4 Request Size and Pagination
The Crossref connector uses offset-based retrieval and exposes it through LitSearch as page-based navigation.
The connector limits an individual Crossref request to a maximum of 100 rows.
This value is a request page-size constraint.
It should not be interpreted as:
• a maximum number of Crossref records that exist for a query
• an All Sources candidate limit
• an export limit
• a global LitSearch result-window limit

The active page and requested page size determine the corresponding Crossref offset.

D.5 Pagination Capabilities
Crossref is represented as a page-based source in the LitSearch capability registry.
The current integration supports:
• previous-page navigation
• last-page navigation
• direct page jumping

These capabilities describe the LitSearch navigation model implemented around Crossref retrieval.
Repeated requests remain dependent on the current state of the external Crossref database and should not be interpreted as access to a permanently frozen upstream result set.

D.6 Publication-Year Filtering
Crossref supports upstream publication-date filtering.
LitSearch translates publication-year boundaries into Crossref filter parameters.
The current connector can use:
• from-pub-date
• until-pub-date

depending on the supplied lower and upper year boundaries.
Conceptually:
year_min
→
from-pub-date

year_max
→
until-pub-date

These restrictions are applied to the Crossref request rather than being implemented solely by discarding records from an already retrieved page.

D.7 Abstract-Availability Filtering
Crossref supports an upstream abstract-presence filter in the current LitSearch integration.
When abstract availability is requested, the connector applies:
has-abstract:true
through the Crossref filter mechanism.
This constrains the Crossref request before the returned records are normalized.
The filter indicates the presence of abstract metadata according to Crossref's indexed metadata.
It does not imply that all abstracts have equivalent structure, completeness, formatting, or quality.

D.8 Sorting
The current Crossref integration supports three LitSearch sort modes:
• relevance
• date_desc
• date_asc

For relevance retrieval, LitSearch uses Crossref's default relevance-oriented behavior.
For chronological retrieval, the connector translates LitSearch ordering into Crossref publication-date sorting.
Conceptually:
date_desc
→
published, descending
date_asc
→
published, ascending

The capability registry therefore exposes relevance and both chronological directions for Crossref.

D.9 Canonical Metadata Mapping
Crossref works are transformed into canonical Paper records.
Depending on metadata availability, normalized fields can include:
• id
• source
• title
• authors
• journal
• year
• publication_date
• abstract
• doi
• url

Crossref metadata do not automatically populate every canonical Paper field.
In particular, the current Crossref normalization does not provide LitSearch MeSH terms or equivalent controlled-vocabulary retrieval metadata.
Fields for which suitable source metadata are unavailable remain empty or unset.

D.10 DOI Handling
DOI is particularly important within the Crossref integration.
Where Crossref supplies a DOI, LitSearch normalizes it into the canonical doi field.
The DOI can subsequently support:
• detail retrieval
• cross-source duplicate detection
• metadata identity
• eligibility for DOI-dependent enrichment providers

A DOI obtained from Crossref does not by itself imply that the associated record contains complete metadata in every canonical field.

D.11 Abstract Metadata
Crossref can return abstract metadata for some records.
Where suitable abstract content is present, the connector normalizes it into the canonical abstract field.
Crossref abstract coverage is not complete across all indexed works.
The availability of an abstract should therefore be interpreted as source metadata availability rather than as a property guaranteed for all Crossref-indexed publications.

D.12 MeSH and Subject Semantics
Crossref does not provide LitSearch MeSH filtering.
Its capability declaration therefore marks MeSH filtering as unsupported.
LitSearch does not attempt to translate MeSH descriptors into generic Crossref bibliographic search terms and present that behavior as equivalent MeSH retrieval.
This maintains the distinction between:
• controlled-vocabulary retrieval in compatible biomedical databases
and
• multidisciplinary bibliographic metadata retrieval through Crossref

D.13 Participation in All Sources
Crossref is one of the six current retrieval sources participating in All Sources.
The current source set is:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

During a new All Sources search, the federated pipeline requests a bounded Crossref candidate set.
Applicable search state propagated to Crossref includes:
• free-text query
• publication-year boundaries
• abstract-availability requirement

MeSH parameters are not propagated as Crossref MeSH filters because the connector does not support that capability.

D.14 Bounded Candidate Retrieval
All Sources does not attempt to exhaustively retrieve every Crossref match.
Instead, the Crossref connector contributes a bounded candidate set determined by the federated candidate-retrieval workflow.
This distinction is important because Crossref queries can represent very large result populations.
The Crossref-reported total should therefore not be interpreted as the number of Crossref records actually inspected by LitSearch during an All Sources request.
The federated workflow operates on the retrieved candidate subset.

D.15 Cross-Source Deduplication
Crossref records participate in centralized All Sources deduplication.
DOI is especially useful for matching Crossref records with records retrieved from other scholarly databases.
The centralized algorithm prioritizes normalized persistent identifiers:
1. DOI
2. PMID
3. PMCID

Where direct identifier matching is unavailable, the shared deduplication process can additionally use bibliographic metadata such as:
• normalized title
• normalized first author
• publication year

A constrained one-year publication-year tolerance can be used under the safeguards defined by the central deduplication implementation.
Persistent-identifier conflicts protect records from inappropriate metadata-based merging.

D.16 Metadata Merging
When a Crossref record is identified as a duplicate of another retrieved record, LitSearch can combine complementary metadata through the central cross-source merge process.
Existing populated values in the primary record are preserved, while missing values can be filled from the matched duplicate where applicable.
Retrieval-source provenance can be combined.
Crossref therefore can contribute bibliographic metadata to a merged Paper even when another source's record remains the primary representation.
This is distinct from metadata enrichment.

D.17 Relevance within All Sources
Crossref's upstream relevance ordering is not numerically normalized against relevance signals from the other five participating sources.
For All Sources relevance-oriented presentation, Crossref candidates retain their relative source-specific candidate order before centralized interleaving.
The current deterministic interleaving order is:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

Crossref's third position in this processing order is not a statement about database quality, evidence quality, or comparative relevance.
It is an implementation rule for deterministic source-balanced presentation.

D.18 Chronological All Sources Ordering
For All Sources date_desc and date_asc, final ordering is performed centrally after candidate retrieval, normalization, deduplication, and metadata merging.
Crossref records therefore participate through their normalized publication metadata rather than through a cross-database comparison of Crossref-specific relevance signals.
This creates a common deterministic chronological ordering over the bounded federated candidate set.

D.19 Detail Retrieval
The Crossref connector supports DOI-based detail retrieval.
Conceptually:
Selected Crossref record
↓
DOI
↓
Crossref work lookup
↓
Canonical Paper
↓
Detail workflow

The resulting canonical Paper can subsequently enter the generic LitSearch metadata-enrichment pipeline.
For example, a Crossref-derived Paper containing a DOI can be eligible for OpenCitations enrichment.
Where applicable, DOI-to-PubMed resolution can also allow PubMed-derived MeSH enrichment.
The resulting enrichment metadata should not be attributed to Crossref merely because Crossref was the original retrieval source.

D.20 Retrieval and Enrichment Provenance
Crossref illustrates the distinction between retrieval provenance and enrichment provenance.

For example:
Crossref search
↓
Crossref Paper with DOI
↓
OpenCitations lookup
↓
citation_count and/or reference_count

In this workflow:
source
→ Crossref retrieval provenance
enrichment_sources
→ OpenCitations provenance for fields actually modified by enrichment

Similarly, PubMed-derived MeSH metadata added to an eligible Crossref record remain enrichment metadata rather than native Crossref MeSH metadata.

D.21 Export
Crossref records can participate in standard LitSearch exports.
Current formats include:
• CSV
• RIS
• XLSX

The export layer operates on normalized Paper records but uses the explicit LitSearch export schema.
CSV and XLSX therefore export the standard nine fields:
• ID
• Source
• Title
• Authors
• Journal
• Year
• DOI
• PMCID
• URL

The complete Crossref API response is not exported.
Similarly, canonical fields outside the current export schema are not automatically included.

D.22 Request Identification
The Crossref connector can use configured contact information in request identification where available.
This can be incorporated into Crossref request metadata such as the User-Agent and mailto parameter.
Such configuration is operational rather than bibliographic.
It does not alter the canonical identity of retrieved records.

D.23 Error Handling
Crossref is an external service and can be affected by:
• network failures
• timeouts
• rate limiting
• upstream server errors
• API changes
• metadata changes

The connector handles these conditions according to the implemented request workflow.
In particular, an HTTP 429 response represents provider-side rate limiting and can result in an empty or unsuccessful connector response rather than a valid literature result set.
Runtime availability should therefore be distinguished from static connector capability.

D.24 Partial Failure within All Sources
A Crossref failure during All Sources candidate retrieval does not necessarily terminate the complete federated search.
The All Sources pipeline isolates source retrieval failures.
If Crossref fails while other connectors succeed, the remaining sources can continue contributing candidates and Crossref can be recorded among the failed sources.
The resulting result set is partial.
A failed Crossref request should not be interpreted as evidence that Crossref contained zero matching records.

D.25 External Metadata Dynamics
Crossref metadata are maintained externally and can change over time.
Changes can include:
• newly registered works
• corrected bibliographic metadata
• DOI metadata updates
• changes in abstract availability
• changes in publication dates
• changes in search ordering

Therefore, repeating the same Crossref query at a later date does not guarantee an identical result set or ordering.
LitSearch can preserve procedural search information, but it cannot freeze the upstream Crossref database.

D.26 Methodological Interpretation
The Crossref integration can be represented as:
Researcher-defined search state
↓
Crossref-specific query translation
↓
Crossref REST API
↓
Bibliographic works
↓
Canonical Paper normalization
↓
Source-specific presentation
or
All Sources candidate aggregation
↓
Central deduplication and metadata merging
↓
Deterministic ordering

For individual detail records, an additional independent path can follow:

Canonical Crossref Paper
↓
Metadata enrichment
↓
Enriched detail record

This architecture preserves the distinction between Crossref as a primary retrieval source and providers that subsequently augment Crossref-derived records.

D.27 Summary
Crossref functions as a multidisciplinary bibliographic retrieval source within LitSearch.
The current integration provides:
• free-text works retrieval
• page- and offset-based navigation
• upstream publication-year filtering
• native abstract-availability filtering
• relevance sorting
• newest-first chronological sorting
• oldest-first chronological sorting
• canonical bibliographic normalization
• DOI-based detail retrieval
• participation in All Sources
• centralized cross-source deduplication
• metadata merging
• compatibility with standard LitSearch exports
• compatibility with applicable DOI-based enrichment workflows

Crossref does not provide LitSearch MeSH retrieval.
Its request page-size constraint should not be confused with the number of results available from Crossref, the All Sources candidate depth, or LitSearch export limits.
Crossref therefore expands multidisciplinary bibliographic coverage while remaining subject to the same principles of explicit capabilities, canonical normalization, deterministic processing, provenance separation, and transparent methodological limitations used throughout LitSearch.

__________________________________
Appendix E — DOAJ Source Profile
This appendix documents the implementation-specific behavior of the DOAJ connector in LitSearch.
DOAJ is integrated as a primary literature retrieval source focused on open-access scholarly journal content. The connector retrieves article metadata through the DOAJ API, translates supported LitSearch search parameters into DOAJ query syntax, and normalizes returned articles into the canonical Paper representation.

E.1 Role within LitSearch
DOAJ functions as:
• an independent primary retrieval source
• a participant in All Sources federated retrieval
• a source of open-access-oriented scholarly metadata
• a source of article keywords and subject metadata
• a source of records for centralized cross-source deduplication
• a source compatible with the shared detail and export architecture

DOAJ is a retrieval source rather than an enrichment provider.
Its inclusion provides an additional discovery route through metadata supplied by the Directory of Open Access Journals.

E.2 API Interface
The current connector uses the DOAJ article-search API.
The principal search resource is:
/api/search/articles
Conceptually:
LitSearch query
↓
DOAJ article search
↓
DOAJ article metadata
↓
Canonical Paper normalization

Individual DOAJ article records can additionally be retrieved through the corresponding article-detail API using the DOAJ article identifier.

E.3 Free-Text Retrieval
A LitSearch free-text query is translated into a DOAJ article-search request.
DOAJ performs the upstream matching and determines the order of the returned search results.
LitSearch normalizes the returned articles but does not attempt to reconstruct an independent DOAJ relevance score.
The current DOAJ integration exposes relevance-oriented retrieval rather than additional chronological sort modes.

E.4 Pagination
DOAJ is represented as a page-based retrieval source in the LitSearch capability model.
The connector uses page and page-size parameters to retrieve the requested result page.
The current integration supports:
• previous-page navigation
• last-page navigation
• direct page jumping

The maximum page size used by the connector is 100 records per request.
This value represents a request-size constraint and should not be interpreted as the total number of DOAJ results available for a query.

E.5 Result-Window Metadata
The current LitSearch capability registry records a maximum result window of 1000 for DOAJ.
This metadata is distinct from the connector's maximum request page size of 100.
Therefore:
maximum request page size
≠
maximum result window

The result-window capability is also distinct from:
• the number of records matching a DOAJ query
• the All Sources candidate depth
• LitSearch export limits

These constraints represent different layers of the retrieval architecture.

E.6 Publication-Year Filtering
DOAJ supports publication-year filtering in the current LitSearch integration.
The connector translates the requested year boundaries into DOAJ query conditions using the article metadata year field.
The relevant source field is:
bibjson.year
Year restrictions are incorporated into the DOAJ search query before results are returned to LitSearch.
This is therefore a server-side retrieval restriction rather than solely a local post-fetch filter.

E.7 Abstract-Availability Filtering
DOAJ supports server-side abstract-availability filtering in the current connector.
When an abstract is required, the query can include an existence condition for:
bibjson.abstract

Conceptually, the connector uses an expression equivalent to:
_exists_:bibjson.abstract
The resulting search is therefore restricted to DOAJ records for which the relevant abstract metadata field exists.
This does not guarantee equivalent abstract completeness, structure, or quality across all returned records.

E.8 Sorting
The current DOAJ integration exposes relevance sorting only.
Its capability declaration therefore contains:
• relevance

and does not expose:
• date_desc
• date_asc

as supported DOAJ sort modes.
The connector does not synthesize unsupported chronological sorting by presenting locally reordered pages as if they represented globally chronological DOAJ retrieval.
This distinction preserves the semantics of the source-specific result set.

E.9 Canonical Metadata Mapping
DOAJ article records are transformed into canonical Paper objects.
Depending on metadata availability, normalized fields can include:
• id
• source
• title
• authors
• journal
• year
• abstract
• doi
• url
• has_full_text
• concepts

The current DOAJ normalization does not populate every canonical field.
For example, publication_date and pmcid can remain unset where no suitable normalized source value is available.
DOAJ does not populate canonical MeSH terms through the current connector.

E.10 DOI Metadata
Where a DOAJ article contains a DOI, the identifier is normalized into the canonical doi field.
This DOI can subsequently contribute to:
• cross-source duplicate detection
• bibliographic identity
• DOI-dependent detail or enrichment workflows

DOAJ records without a DOI can still participate in LitSearch retrieval and can potentially be compared through the metadata-based stages of centralized deduplication.

E.11 Full-Text Metadata
DOAJ article metadata can include links associated with article access.
Where the connector can derive suitable information from those metadata, the canonical Paper can represent full-text availability through:
has_full_text
and an appropriate URL.
This field indicates metadata-derived availability within the LitSearch representation.
It should not be interpreted as a guarantee that LitSearch has downloaded, archived, or independently verified the full text of the publication.

E.12 Concepts
The current DOAJ normalization can derive canonical concepts from DOAJ article metadata.
Relevant values can include:
• keywords
• subject metadata

These values can be combined into the canonical concepts field.
DOAJ concepts remain source-derived descriptive metadata.
They should not be interpreted as equivalent to MeSH descriptors.

Therefore:
DOAJ keywords and subjects
≠
MeSH terms

E.13 MeSH Capability
DOAJ does not support LitSearch MeSH filtering.
The capability registry therefore marks MeSH filtering as unsupported for DOAJ.
LitSearch does not transform MeSH descriptors into DOAJ keywords or subject labels and present the resulting search as controlled-vocabulary-equivalent retrieval.
When MeSH is used in a federated workflow, DOAJ retains its own retrieval semantics.

E.14 Detail Retrieval
The DOAJ connector supports article-detail retrieval through the DOAJ article API.

Conceptually:
Selected DOAJ result
↓
DOAJ article identifier
↓
DOAJ article-detail request
↓
Canonical Paper
↓
Detail workflow

The normalized detail record can subsequently enter the generic LitSearch enrichment pipeline where applicable.
The DOAJ retrieval operation and any later enrichment remain separate methodological stages.

E.15 Participation in All Sources
DOAJ is one of the six current sources participating in the All Sources candidate-retrieval pipeline.
The current source set is:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

During All Sources retrieval, DOAJ receives a bounded candidate request.
Applicable propagated search state includes:
• free-text query
• publication-year restrictions
• abstract-availability requirement

MeSH parameters are not translated into a DOAJ MeSH filter because DOAJ does not support that capability.

E.16 Bounded Candidate Retrieval
All Sources does not attempt to retrieve the complete DOAJ result population.
Instead, DOAJ contributes a bounded candidate set to the federated pipeline.
The size of that candidate set is determined by the All Sources retrieval workflow and relevant application configuration, subject to connector and upstream constraints.

Consequently:
DOAJ total matching population
≠
DOAJ candidates inspected by an All Sources request

This limitation is shared conceptually with the other federated sources.

E.17 Cross-Source Deduplication
DOAJ records participate in centralized cross-source deduplication.
Where available, DOI provides a strong basis for matching a DOAJ article to corresponding records retrieved from other sources.
The central deduplication process prioritizes normalized persistent identifiers:
1. DOI
2. PMID
3. PMCID

When direct identifier matching is unavailable, bibliographic metadata can additionally contribute to duplicate detection.
These metadata can include:
• normalized title
• normalized first author
• publication year

A constrained one-year publication-year tolerance can be used under the safeguards implemented by the central deduplication algorithm.
Conflicting persistent identifiers prevent inappropriate metadata-based merging.

E.18 Metadata Merging
When a DOAJ article is matched with a duplicate from another retrieval source, the centralized merge process can combine complementary metadata.
The primary record retains existing populated values.
Missing values can be filled from the matched duplicate where appropriate.
Retrieval-source provenance can be combined.
This means, for example, that useful DOAJ metadata can contribute to a merged Paper even when another source supplied the primary representation.
Cross-source metadata merging remains distinct from provider-based metadata enrichment.

E.19 Relevance within All Sources
DOAJ does not expose an independent chronological sort mode through the current LitSearch connector.
For All Sources relevance-oriented retrieval, DOAJ contributes records in its source-specific candidate order.
LitSearch does not attempt to normalize a DOAJ relevance signal numerically against those of the other participating databases.
After aggregation and deduplication, the source buckets are interleaved according to the deterministic order:
1. PubMed
2. OpenAlex
3. Crossref
4. DOAJ
5. Europe PMC
6. Semantic Scholar

DOAJ's fourth position is an implementation rule for deterministic source-balanced interleaving.
It is not an assessment of source quality or scientific importance.

E.20 Chronological All Sources Ordering
Although DOAJ does not expose source-specific date_desc or date_asc modes in LitSearch, DOAJ records can still participate in chronological All Sources ordering.
This is possible because the federated workflow performs central ordering after candidate retrieval, normalization, deduplication, and metadata merging.

Conceptually:
DOAJ relevance-oriented candidates
↓
Canonical year metadata
↓
All Sources candidate aggregation
↓
Deduplication and merging
↓
Central date ordering

This illustrates an important distinction between:
source-specific sorting capability
and
federated post-aggregation ordering

The two should not be treated as equivalent.

E.21 Retrieval and Enrichment Provenance
A DOAJ-derived Paper can subsequently receive metadata from an enrichment provider if the record satisfies the provider's matching requirements.

For example:
DOAJ Paper with DOI
↓
OpenCitations
↓
citation_count and/or reference_count

In such a case:
source
→ DOAJ retrieval provenance
enrichment_sources
→ provenance for metadata actually added by the enrichment provider

Likewise, an eligible DOI-associated record could potentially receive PubMed-derived MeSH metadata through the implemented DOI-to-PubMed enrichment path.
Such MeSH metadata would be enrichment metadata and would not mean that DOAJ itself supports MeSH retrieval.

E.22 Export
DOAJ records can participate in standard LitSearch exports.
Current formats include:
• CSV
• RIS
• XLSX

CSV and XLSX use the standard nine-field export schema:
• ID
• Source
• Title
• Authors
• Journal
• Year
• DOI
• PMCID
• URL

Consequently, DOAJ-specific subject metadata and canonical concepts are not automatically included in these exports.
Likewise, the complete upstream DOAJ record is not serialized into the standard export files.

E.23 Error Handling
The DOAJ connector explicitly handles unsuccessful API interactions through connector-level error behavior.
Possible operational failures include:
• network errors
• timeouts
• upstream service errors
• invalid responses
• API changes

Such failures should be distinguished from a successful DOAJ request containing no matching records.
Static capability declarations describe intended connector functionality; they do not guarantee that the external DOAJ service is reachable at runtime.

E.24 Partial Failure within All Sources
A DOAJ failure during All Sources retrieval does not necessarily terminate the complete federated request.
Source calls are isolated within the federated candidate-retrieval layer.
If DOAJ fails, the remaining sources can continue contributing candidates and DOAJ can be recorded among the failed sources.
The resulting result set is therefore partial.
A failed DOAJ source call must not be interpreted as equivalent to a valid zero-result response.

E.25 External Metadata Dynamics
DOAJ is externally maintained.
Its indexed records and metadata can change over time as journals and article metadata are added, corrected, or updated.
Repeated retrieval at different dates can therefore produce differences in:
• matching records
• metadata values
• abstract availability
• DOI availability
• subject metadata
• full-text link metadata
• result ordering

LitSearch can document the retrieval procedure but cannot guarantee temporal immutability of the external database.

E.26 Methodological Interpretation
The DOAJ integration can be represented as:
Researcher-defined search state
↓
DOAJ-specific query construction
↓
DOAJ article-search API
↓
DOAJ article metadata
↓
Canonical Paper normalization
↓
Source-specific presentation
or
All Sources candidate aggregation
↓
Central deduplication and metadata merging
↓
Deterministic ordering

For individual records:
Canonical DOAJ Paper
↓
Optional applicable metadata enrichment
↓
Enriched detail record

This architecture preserves DOAJ-specific retrieval behavior while allowing normalized DOAJ records to participate in shared LitSearch workflows.

E.27 Summary
DOAJ functions as an open-access-oriented primary retrieval source within LitSearch.

The current integration provides:
• free-text article retrieval
• page-based navigation
• server-side publication-year filtering
• server-side abstract-availability filtering
• relevance-oriented source-specific retrieval
• canonical Paper normalization
• DOI metadata where available
• abstract metadata where available
• full-text availability metadata where derivable
• keyword and subject metadata normalized as concepts
• source-specific article-detail retrieval
• participation in All Sources
• centralized cross-source deduplication
• metadata merging
• compatibility with standard LitSearch exports
• compatibility with applicable metadata-enrichment workflows

DOAJ does not support LitSearch MeSH filtering or source-specific chronological sorting.
Its request page-size constraint, result-window metadata, federated candidate depth, and export limits represent separate architectural constraints and should not be conflated.
The DOAJ connector therefore extends LitSearch with an additional open-access-oriented discovery source while preserving source-specific retrieval semantics and the shared principles of normalization, provenance, deterministic processing, and transparent limitations.

________________________________________
Appendix F — Metadata Enrichment Providers
This appendix documents the metadata-enrichment architecture and the enrichment providers currently integrated into LitSearch.
Metadata enrichment is methodologically distinct from primary literature retrieval.
Retrieval sources identify publications and create canonical Paper records.
Enrichment providers operate on already retrieved Paper records and can add supplementary metadata without introducing additional publications into the retrieval result set.

F.1 Role of Metadata Enrichment
The enrichment layer extends the metadata associated with an existing canonical Paper.

Conceptually:
Primary retrieval
↓
Canonical Paper
↓
Metadata enrichment
↓
Enriched canonical Paper

The enrichment stage does not redefine the original search query and does not independently contribute new papers to the search-result population.

This distinction allows LitSearch to expand record-level metadata without conflating bibliographic discovery with subsequent metadata augmentation.

F.2 Retrieval versus Enrichment
LitSearch distinguishes two provider categories.
Retrieval sources currently include:
• PubMed
• Europe PMC
• OpenAlex
• Semantic Scholar
• Crossref
• DOAJ

Current enrichment functionality includes:
• PubMed-derived MeSH metadata enrichment
• OpenCitations citation and reference count enrichment

The distinction can be summarized as:
Retrieval source
→ identifies and retrieves a publication record
Enrichment provider
→ augments an already retrieved publication record

A service can therefore contribute metadata to LitSearch without becoming a participant in All Sources retrieval.

F.3 Generic Enrichment Architecture
The enrichment subsystem is designed around provider-independent processing.

Conceptually:
Paper
↓
Configured enrichment provider
↓
EnrichmentResult
↓
Generic merge logic
↓
Updated Paper

The provider is responsible for obtaining supplementary metadata.
The generic merge layer determines how supported metadata are incorporated into the canonical Paper.
This separation prevents each enrichment provider from independently redefining canonical merge behavior.

F.4 Enrichment Results
An enrichment provider returns an enrichment result describing whether the provider matched the Paper and which supplementary metadata were obtained.
An unmatched result does not modify the Paper.
A matched result can supply metadata that are subsequently evaluated by the generic merge layer.
Provider matching and metadata merging are therefore separate operations.
This distinction is important because successfully contacting a provider does not necessarily imply that the canonical Paper will be changed.

F.5 Provider Execution
Configured enrichment providers are processed sequentially within the enrichment pipeline, with provider-specific failures isolated from primary retrieval.

Conceptually:
Retrieved Paper
↓
Provider A
↓
Provider B
↓
Merged enrichment metadata
↓
Paper returned to application

If an enrichment provider fails, the underlying retrieved Paper remains valid.
Enrichment availability is therefore not a prerequisite for successful primary literature retrieval.

F.6 Current Runtime Placement
In the current LitSearch implementation, metadata enrichment is used primarily in the individual paper-detail workflow.

Conceptually:
Source-specific detail retrieval
↓
Canonical Paper
↓
Detail enrichment pipeline
↓
Enriched Paper
↓
Detail-page presentation

The currently configured detail enrichment providers are:
• PubMed-derived MeSH enrichment
• OpenCitations enrichment

The enrichment pipeline should not be interpreted as a mandatory stage for every search-result list or standard export.

F.7 Supported Enrichment Fields
The generic enrichment merge layer currently recognizes selected canonical fields.

Supported list fields include:
• mesh_terms
• concepts

Supported scalar fields include:
• abstract
• citation_count
• reference_count

The existence of a field in the canonical Paper does not automatically mean that every enrichment provider is permitted to modify it.
Metadata outside the supported enrichment merge set are ignored by the generic merge operation.

F.8 Scalar Merge Semantics
For supported scalar fields, LitSearch preserves an existing populated canonical value.
An enrichment value is used to fill the field only when the corresponding canonical value is empty.

Conceptually:
existing scalar value present
→ preserve existing value
existing scalar value empty
+
provider value available
→ fill from provider

This behavior reduces unnecessary overwriting of metadata already obtained through primary retrieval or earlier processing.

F.9 List Merge Semantics
Supported list fields can combine existing canonical metadata with values supplied through enrichment.
The merge process performs case-insensitive duplicate detection while preserving deterministic order and existing value representation where possible.

Conceptually:
existing list
+
provider list
↓
case-insensitive duplicate detection
↓
ordered combined list

This is particularly relevant to controlled or descriptive metadata such as MeSH terms and concepts.

F.10 Unsupported and Identity Fields
The generic enrichment merge layer does not allow arbitrary provider metadata to overwrite the complete canonical Paper.
Fields outside the supported enrichment merge set are ignored.
This protects identity and retrieval-related metadata from unintended provider-side modification.
Enrichment is therefore constrained augmentation rather than unrestricted record replacement.

F.11 Enrichment Provenance
The canonical Paper contains:
enrichment_sources
This field records provenance for metadata fields actually changed through enrichment.

Conceptually:
enrichment_sources[field]
→ provider or providers that contributed to that enriched field

Provider provenance is added only when the merge operation changes the canonical Paper.
A provider that returns no usable change should not be recorded as though it supplied the resulting field value.

F.12 Retrieval Provenance versus Enrichment Provenance
LitSearch keeps retrieval provenance and enrichment provenance conceptually separate.

For example:
source = "crossref"

can indicate that the publication record was retrieved through Crossref.
If OpenCitations subsequently adds citation_count, the enrichment provenance can indicate that OpenCitations supplied that field.

Therefore:
source
→ origin of retrieval record
enrichment_sources
→ provenance of metadata fields changed by enrichment

The enrichment_sources field is not intended as a complete provenance graph for all canonical metadata.

F.13 PubMed-Derived MeSH Enrichment
LitSearch includes a PubMed-based enrichment provider for MeSH metadata.
The purpose of this provider is to obtain MeSH descriptor metadata for an already retrieved Paper.
This functionality should be distinguished from PubMed MeSH search refinement.

PubMed MeSH retrieval:
researcher-supplied MeSH descriptors
↓
modify search query
↓
affect which publications are retrieved

PubMed MeSH enrichment:
already retrieved Paper
↓
identify corresponding PubMed record
↓
retrieve assigned MeSH metadata
↓
augment Paper

The two workflows use MeSH for different methodological purposes.

F.14 PubMed Record Matching
For a Paper originating from PubMed, the PubMed identifier represented by the record can be used directly for enrichment.
For suitable non-PubMed records, the provider can use DOI-based resolution to identify a corresponding PubMed record.

Conceptually:
PubMed-derived Paper
→ PubMed identifier
→ PubMed metadata

or:
Non-PubMed Paper with DOI
→ DOI-to-PubMed resolution
→ corresponding PubMed record
→ MeSH metadata

A successful DOI-to-PubMed match can therefore allow MeSH metadata to enrich a Paper originally retrieved from another source.

F.15 MeSH Enrichment Output
When a suitable PubMed record is identified, assigned MeSH descriptor terms can be returned as enrichment metadata.
These terms can be merged into:
mesh_terms
according to the generic list-field merge rules.
Where the merge changes the canonical Paper, provenance is associated with the PubMed enrichment source.
This does not change the Paper's original retrieval source.

F.16 Interpretation of Enriched MeSH Metadata
MeSH metadata obtained through enrichment describe indexing associated with the matched PubMed record.
They should not be interpreted as evidence that the original retrieval source supports MeSH searching.

For example:
Crossref Paper
↓
DOI-to-PubMed match
↓
PubMed MeSH enrichment

does not imply:
Crossref supports MeSH retrieval
Likewise, enriching a DOAJ or OpenAlex record with PubMed MeSH metadata does not alter the declared MeSH capability of those retrieval connectors.

F.17 OpenCitations Enrichment
LitSearch includes an OpenCitations enrichment provider for citation-related metadata.
OpenCitations operates as an enrichment provider rather than a primary literature retrieval source.
The current provider requires a DOI for the Paper being enriched.

Conceptually:
Canonical Paper with DOI
↓
OpenCitations lookup
↓
citation/reference metadata
↓
Generic enrichment merge
↓
Enriched Paper

A Paper without a suitable DOI cannot use this DOI-dependent enrichment path.

F.18 Citation Count
Where available, OpenCitations can provide a citation count.
The value can populate:
citation_count
when the canonical field is empty and the enrichment result supplies a usable value.
The count represents metadata obtained from the enrichment provider.
It should not be interpreted as an independently calculated LitSearch citation count.

F.19 Reference Count
Where available, OpenCitations can also provide a reference count.
The value can populate:
reference_count
under the applicable scalar merge rules.
Citation count and reference count are handled independently.
Availability of one value does not require availability of the other.
This allows partial OpenCitations metadata to remain useful when only one count is returned.

F.20 Interpretation of Citation Metadata
Citation and reference counts are provider-derived metadata.

They should not be interpreted as direct measures of:
• methodological quality
• evidential strength
• scientific validity
• clinical importance
• relevance to a specific research question

Citation metadata can be useful descriptive information, but LitSearch does not transform these counts into quality scores or evidence rankings.

F.21 OpenCitations Provenance
When OpenCitations supplies a value that changes the canonical Paper, the corresponding field can be associated with OpenCitations through enrichment_sources.

For example:
citation_count
→ OpenCitations
reference_count
→ OpenCitations

where those values were actually added through the enrichment operation.
This field-level provenance distinguishes enrichment metadata from bibliographic metadata obtained during primary retrieval.

F.22 Provider Failure Isolation
External enrichment providers can be unavailable or return incomplete information.

Potential causes include:
• network failures
• timeouts
• upstream service errors
• identifier mismatch
• incomplete provider coverage
• API changes

Such failures should not invalidate the primary Paper.

Conceptually:
successful retrieval
+
failed enrichment
=
valid retrieved Paper without the unavailable enrichment

This is a core architectural property of the enrichment layer.

F.23 Partial Enrichment
Enrichment is not an all-or-nothing process.
A provider can return only part of the metadata it potentially supports.
For example, OpenCitations may supply one count while another remains unavailable.
Similarly, one enrichment provider can succeed while another fails or does not match the Paper.

The final Paper can therefore contain a mixture of:
• primary retrieval metadata
• cross-source merged metadata
• metadata from one enrichment provider
• metadata from another enrichment provider
• unavailable fields

The canonical model is designed to accommodate this heterogeneity.

F.24 Enrichment and Cross-Source Deduplication
Metadata enrichment and cross-source deduplication are separate processing concepts.
Cross-source deduplication operates on retrieved candidate records.
Its purpose is to identify multiple retrieved representations of the same publication and consolidate them.
Enrichment operates on an already selected canonical Paper.
Its purpose is to obtain supplementary metadata from an external provider.

Conceptually:
Retrieval A + Retrieval B
↓
duplicate detection
↓
cross-source metadata merge
↓
canonical Paper

is different from:
canonical Paper
↓
enrichment provider
↓
supplementary metadata
↓
enriched Paper

Keeping these operations separate makes provenance and processing behavior easier to interpret.

F.25 Enrichment and All Sources
Enrichment providers are not additional All Sources candidate sources.

The current All Sources retrieval set consists of:
• PubMed
• OpenAlex
• Crossref
• DOAJ
• Europe PMC
• Semantic Scholar

OpenCitations does not contribute a seventh candidate stream.
Similarly, PubMed MeSH enrichment does not represent an additional PubMed retrieval pass that adds publications to the federated result population.
Enrichment therefore does not change the number of publications in the result set merely by being configured.

F.26 Enrichment and Export
The canonical Paper can contain enriched fields that are not included in the current standard CSV, XLSX, or RIS export schemas.
Current CSV and XLSX exports contain:
• ID
• Source
• Title
• Authors
• Journal
• Year
• DOI
• PMCID
• URL

RIS likewise exports a defined bibliographic subset and does not currently include the enrichment-specific fields described above.

Therefore, fields such as:
• mesh_terms
• concepts
• citation_count
• reference_count
• enrichment_sources

are not automatically exported through the current standard CSV and XLSX schemas.
The existence of enriched metadata in a detail workflow should not therefore be interpreted as evidence that the same metadata are present in a generated export.

F.27 Enrichment Caching Boundary
The enrichment architecture defines a cache-compatible abstraction for enrichment processing.
However, the existence of an enrichment cache interface should not be interpreted as evidence that all enrichment responses are currently stored through an active persistent enrichment cache.
The methods description therefore distinguishes architectural support for caching from verified runtime use of a concrete enrichment cache implementation.
This prevents implementation interfaces from being mistaken for deployed methodological behavior.

F.28 Adding a New Enrichment Provider
A future enrichment provider should integrate with the generic enrichment architecture rather than behaving as an implicit retrieval source.

A new provider should define, where applicable:
1. matching requirements
2. required identifiers
3. provider request behavior
4. fields supplied
5. matched and unmatched behavior
6. failure handling
7. compatibility with generic merge rules
8. provenance identity
9. automated validation

If a new provider requires additional canonical fields, those fields should be introduced explicitly into the Paper model and corresponding merge behavior.
Adding an enrichment provider should not silently alter primary retrieval semantics.

F.29 Validation Requirements
Enrichment functionality should be validated independently from retrieval-source behavior.

Relevant validation includes:
• matched-provider behavior
• unmatched-provider behavior
• scalar merge semantics
• list merge semantics
• preservation of existing values
• duplicate suppression in list fields
• provenance recording
• provider failure isolation
• identifier-dependent matching
• partial metadata responses

This separation allows enrichment defects to be detected without conflating them with connector retrieval defects.

F.30 Methodological Interpretation
The complete enrichment model can be represented as:
Primary retrieval source
↓
Canonical Paper
↓
Optional cross-source consolidation
↓
Detail retrieval
↓
Configured enrichment providers
↓
Provider-specific matching
↓
EnrichmentResult
↓
Controlled generic merge
↓
Field-level enrichment provenance
↓
Enriched detail record

The enrichment layer augments bibliographic context while preserving the identity and provenance of the underlying retrieved publication.

F.31 Summary
LitSearch implements metadata enrichment as a separate architectural layer from primary literature retrieval.
The current enrichment functionality includes:
• PubMed-derived MeSH descriptor enrichment
• OpenCitations citation-count enrichment
• OpenCitations reference-count enrichment

The generic enrichment layer provides:
• provider-independent processing
• controlled scalar-field merging
• controlled list-field merging
• preservation of existing populated scalar metadata
• case-insensitive list deduplication
• field-level enrichment provenance
• provider failure isolation

Enrichment does not independently add publications to a result set and does not convert an enrichment provider into an All Sources retrieval source.
The current runtime use is primarily associated with individual paper-detail processing rather than mandatory enrichment of all search results or standard exports.
This separation preserves a clear methodological boundary between literature identification and subsequent metadata augmentation.
